INFO 03-28 14:00:19 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-4880d0bae4b24435937487abfae4e6a6-0: prompt: 'Continuation of chapter one:\n"2019). Although the parts of the anxiety buffering system are often inter-related, they may still offer slightly other means for terror management. However, when more than one of these anxiety buffers are malfunctioning, people are left defenseless against death-related thoughts and anxiety, which might lead to feelings of helplessness and depression (Mikulincer & Shaver, 2012, Mikulincer et al., 2020). Psychopathology might then arise when early childhood problems, traumatic events, or other disruptive life changes increase the level of threat and potential for debilitating anxiety, which requires the normal functioning of the anxiety buffering system. Thus, from the ABDT perspective, low self-esteem, attachment security, and connection to the cultural worldview are a transdiagnostic contributors to mental disorders (Yetzer & Pyszczynski, 2019). Importantly, understanding the pivotal (and yet dynamic) role that self-esteem plays in the anxiety buffering system may allow psychologists to better treat psychological disorders related to anxiety. \n \n \nSelf-Esteem and Immortality \n \nAs we noted in the beginning of this chapter, from the TMT perspective, a central but rather implicit part of self-esteem is the feeling that one is worthy of protection and immortality. Many studies have found that, in line with TMT, believing that one will enjoy modes of literal or symbolic immortality is associated with lower levels of death anxiety and defensiveness in response to reminders of death (e.g., Dechesne et al., 2003; Florian, & Mikulincer, 1998b; Wojtkowiak & Rutjens, 2011). In addition, there are studies showing that MS increases peoples’ motivation for obtaining symbolic immortality (e.g., Greenberg et al., 2010), and that affirming people’s symbolic legacy or literal immortality beliefs reduces DTA and defensiveness in response to MS (e.g., Dechesne et al., 2003; Wojtkowiak & Rutjens, 2011). Thus, immortality beliefs seem to function as death anxiety buffers, as selfesteem does. \nHowever, it is only recently that research has directly assessed whether self-esteem affords people a sense of symbolic immortality. Specifically, Lifshin et al. (2021) conducted several large-scale correlational studies with American college students, in which they included measures of self-esteem (either a short 1-item measure of selfesteem, or the 10-item Rosenberg self-esteem scale), and several measures of symbolic immortality (e.g., “Do you think that you will be remembered by other people after you die [e.g., through your \naccomplishments or legacy]?”; “After I die, my impact on the world will continue and be remembered by others”). The researchers also included a measure assessing the participants’ belief that they may enjoy literal immortality (“Do you think that you will live again after you die [e.g., in heaven or through reincarnation?]”), as well as other measures related to terror management and self-esteem such as: in-group identification, loneliness, existential isolation, attachment insecurities, perceived meaning in life, DTA, and depression. \nIn line with the TMT of self-esteem, findings across seven samples indicated that self-esteem was consistently related to believing that one would be remembered and have an impact after they die. Furthermore, this relationship remained strong after statistically controlling for the participants’ levels of loneliness (social connectedness), attachment insecurities, and perceived meaning in life (which were all related to both symbolic immortality and to self-esteem), as well as other variables. This point is important because the findings eliminate these other variables as alternative reasons for why self-esteem predicts belief in one’s symbolic immortality, for example the idea that self-esteem just does so by increasing a sense of social connection. \nFindings from this research also supported the self-esteem and immortality hypothesis regarding literal immortality, although this relationship was weaker than the one between self-esteem and symbolic immortality. Unlike with symbolic immortality, however, self-esteem was only significantly related to a greater sense of literal immortality among participants who believe that an afterlife exists (not among those who do not). \nFinally, the authors conducted mediational analyses showing that participants’ sense of symbolic immortality partially mediated the effect of self-esteem on DTA and depression, as higher levels of self-esteem were associated with higher beliefs in symbolic immortality, which in turn was negatively associated with both DTA and depression. The finding that this mediational effect was only partial suggests that the selfesteem may buffer against death awareness by both providing a more \n“direct” solution to death (i.e., belief in symbolic immortality) or though raising the degree to which people feel protected more generally. \nThe findings from Lifshin et al. (2021) suggest that feelings of symbolic immortality (or lack thereof) may be an important aspect of self-esteem and the death-anxiety buffering system. However, because these studies were correlational in nature, additional experimental or perhaps longitudinal research is needed to provide stronger support for the notion that higher/lower levels of self-esteem cause higher/lower levels of symbolic immortality. \n \n \nCONCLUSION \n \nIn this chapter we have presented the terror management theory (Greenberg et al. 1986) of self-esteem, which posits that self-esteem functions as a protective psychological resource, shielding people from anxiety in general, as well as from the specific potential for debilitating anxiety resulting from the awareness of mortality. Research reviewed throughout this chapter has supported this terror management function of self-esteem by showing that (1) reminders of death increase self-esteem striving, (2) self-esteem reduces defensiveness, anxiety, and death-related thought in response to reminders of mortality, (3) threats to self-esteem increase death-related cognition, (4) low self-esteem is associated with deficient terror management mechanisms and results in death anxiety and risk for psychopathology after trauma, and (5) high self-esteem is associated with a stronger sense of immortality. \nThe role of self-esteem underlying much of human behavior has been a central theme in psychological theorizing (e.g., Crocker & Wolfe, 2001; Horney, 1937; James, 1890; Leary & Baumeister, 2000; Mead, 1934; Rosenberg, 1965; Sullivan, 1953), and psychological research has borne important findings regarding the importance of self-esteem in psychological functioning (e.g., Orth & Robins, 2014). Illuminating the terror management function of self-esteem has had broad implications for understanding, predicting and modifying human thought and behavior, and research within this framework has advanced psychological research in various domains (for reviews, see Routledge & Vess, 2019; Solomon et al., 2015). We should note that although TMT views self-esteem as driven primarily by defensive needs, it does not argue that these needs are the only ones underlying human behavior or self-esteem, and acknowledges the role of other motivations to acquire self-esteem, such as growth and enrichment motives (e.g., Greenberg et al., 1995). Still, given the evidence reviewed, we believe that terror management theory of self-esteem is critical for understanding what self-esteem is and why it matters. \n \n \nREFERENCES \n \nAbeyta, A. A., Juhl, J., & Routledge, C. (2014). Exploring the effects of self-esteem and mortality salience on proximal and distally measured death anxiety: A further test of the dual process model of terror management. Motivation and Emotion, 38, 523-528. https://doi.org/ 10.1007/s11031-014-9400-y. \nArndt, J., Greenberg, J., Schimel, J., Pyszczynski, T., & Solomon, S. (2002). To belong or not to belong, that is the question: Terror management and identification with gender and ethnicity. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 83, 26–43. https://doi.org/10. 1037/0022-3514.83.1.26. \n"', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [36139, 4002, 315, 12453, 825, 510, 1, 17, 15, 16, 24, 568, 10328, 279, 5479, 315, 279, 18056, 87139, 1849, 525, 3545, 946, 13904, 11, 807, 1231, 2058, 3010, 10078, 1008, 3363, 369, 8648, 6240, 13, 4354, 11, 979, 803, 1091, 825, 315, 1493, 18056, 27389, 525, 71187, 287, 11, 1251, 525, 2115, 9055, 1717, 2348, 4545, 13904, 11303, 323, 18056, 11, 892, 2578, 2990, 311, 15650, 315, 1492, 31928, 323, 18210, 320, 44, 1579, 360, 69798, 609, 1417, 7255, 11, 220, 17, 15, 16, 17, 11, 42242, 360, 69798, 1842, 452, 2572, 220, 17, 15, 17, 15, 568, 17235, 35111, 2449, 2578, 1221, 30789, 979, 4124, 19990, 5322, 11, 53186, 4357, 11, 476, 1008, 61542, 2272, 4344, 5263, 279, 2188, 315, 5899, 323, 4650, 369, 91790, 18056, 11, 892, 7460, 279, 4622, 30201, 315, 279, 18056, 87139, 1849, 13, 14301, 11, 504, 279, 362, 9548, 51, 13057, 11, 3347, 656, 64806, 11, 19984, 4763, 11, 323, 3633, 311, 279, 12752, 77062, 525, 264, 1356, 8579, 11953, 20343, 311, 10502, 23741, 320, 28074, 7070, 609, 393, 72345, 13769, 1872, 32539, 11, 220, 17, 15, 16, 24, 568, 13213, 17548, 11, 8660, 279, 59750, 320, 437, 3602, 8741, 8, 3476, 429, 656, 64806, 11088, 304, 279, 18056, 87139, 1849, 1231, 2138, 73091, 311, 2664, 4228, 23187, 23741, 5435, 311, 18056, 13, 31906, 715, 12092, 13431, 5342, 336, 323, 15