INFO 03-28 14:00:32 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-3cb7724e727640049c67de4062493e7e-0: prompt: 'Summarize this \n\n[0041] Referring to FIG. 4, illustrated is a representative user interface (as represented by reference numeral 400) for creating a template from an existing contract document, in accordance with one or more exemplary embodiments of the present disclosure. The user interface 400, as provided, may be implemented in the user device 300 as described. In an example, the user interface 400 provides an upload button 402a. A standard document that needs to be templatized is uploaded using the upload button 402a. In other example, an existing template (which may already be stored in a storage arrangement of the system 100) may be imported (via search or the like) to be edited via an edit template button 402b using the proposed framework. Herein, the template may be provided with a desired name using a template name box 404. Thereafter, the document is loaded in a document window 406. It may be appreciated that the contract documents even of the same type may have many fields that may vary from one contract to another, like party name, date of commencement, party address, purpose, jurisdiction, term etc., and thus need to be identified to create a template for the said contract document type. \n[0042] In one embodiment, the fields to be created may be identified in the contact document by manual selection on the loaded document in the document window 406. In such case, a name of each of the manually identified fields is assigned in a fields window 408. Once the fields are created, those may be highlighted in the document. The present user interface 400 with the available fields window 408 may allow to perform the described manual process of identifying fields, which may ultimately be used for creating contract document template, in a user-friendly and intuitive manner, which is not the case with conventionally known contract templatization software and services. \n[0043] In a preferred embodiment, the fields to be created may be identified in the contract document by automatic identification using a machine learning algorithm. The algorithm is based on an NER (named entity recognition) technique. Herein, the user interface 400 may be configured to allow a user to define one or more entities from the displayed text of the contract document in the document window 406; and train a suitable machine learning model to determine entities complementary to the defined entity in the contract document. Specifically, an NER based algorithm is used to extract the entities for the uploaded documents. Some of the entities of interest may include: party1, party2, effective date which are available in the party clause of a contract, contract type, which may, for example, be available in the heading of the document and the term/termination clause of the contract document. \n[0044] In an example, separate models may be created to identify each or a group of these entities by training the corresponding NER models. The NER algorithm is trained by using data with entities in an IOB form (short for inside, outside, beginning), extracted from various documents. The machine learning model may be trained using a variety of models. The process can be iterated multiple times till a desired level of accuracy is achieved. Once the models are trained, tag extraction is carried out on relevant clauses using the inference pipeline. The users can extract information of their interest using this framework. This is an intuitive and quick way for users to be able to extract custom information from text data.\n[0045] Referring to FIG. 5, illustrated is a schematic of a modelling pipeline (as represented by reference numeral 500) for generating an NER model, as per embodiments of the present disclosure. As shown, at step 502, the modelling pipeline 500 involves using a training corpus of various contract documents types (as identified). In the present examples, the training corpus dataset may need a large number of documents, typically more than 600 per entity, in order to deal with data imbalance. At step 504, the modelling pipeline 500 involves using an annotation tool, such as Prodigy, Inception, Docanno, Brat, etc., to come up with IOB labels (which are a format for chunks, and are similar to part-of-speech tags but can denote the inside, outside, and beginning of a chunk) for the used training corpus. At step 506, the modelling pipeline 500 involves generating the NER model using the annotated training corpus by implementing machine learning techniques including, but not limited to, CRFs (conditional random fields), BiLSTM (Bidirectional LSTM), CNN, ElMo (Embeddings from Language Model), Stanford NLP, or other transformer-based models (e.g., BERT, GPT3), etc., without any limitations. \n[0046] Further, referring to FIG. 6, illustrated is a schematic of an inference pipeline (as represented by reference numeral 600) for implementing the generated NER model (as described in reference to the modelling pipeline 500 of FIG. 5) for entities identification in the contract document being processed (as in the document window 406). Herein, the inference pipeline 600 involves inputting extracted clauses from the contract documents, as may be achieved by a clause classification/extraction model (not described herein for brevity of the present disclosure). The inference pipeline 600 further involves implementing the NER model as described for recognizing entities in the text (as being represented by block 604 in FIG. 6). In the present implementation, the text is pre-processed as per the requirement of the NER model. Herein, an output of the inference pipeline 600 is in the form of extracted entity details from the contract document being processed (as being represented by block 606 in FIG. 6). As may be appreciated, the entity details may include information like parties, term, jurisdiction, effective date, type of the contract document, etc.\n[0047] Subsequently, the identified entities may be defined as fields for the contract document type. As may be understood that each of the identified entities (which may be converted into fields) may have associated properties (field type), like “date” for effective date, “text” for party name, “integer” for amount, etc. Such properties for the various identified entities may be automatically determined, and the corresponding field types may be set as such. Herein, for instance, the properties of the fields (i.e., field types) may be set as of different types like integer, float, date, text, etc. Further, properties of the fields may be set as a dropdown (for standard lists like jurisdictions, country names, etc.), calendar (for dates), etc. All of this may be done in the fields window 408, as previously described in reference to FIG. 4. In the present embodiments, the user interface 400 may be configured to allow for the field types (as created) to be edited, deleted, or modified, if required.\n[0048] Also, as illustrated in FIG. 4, the user interface 400 provides one or more signature and stamp pads boxes 410a, 410b which may allow inserting signatures/stamps in the document at a desired location. Further, the user interface 400 may be configured to allow the insertion of signatures on certain page(s) or all the pages (as required) of the contract document by use of an insert signature button 412. Also, the user interface 400 may be configured to allow the insertion of signatures on certain page(s) or all the pages (as required) of the contract document by use of an insert stamp pad button 414. In some examples, the user interface 400 may also provide a photograph button 416 which can be added and configured as a photo capture field. The user interface 400 may also allow to define a workflow by identifying various steps involved, setting names/emails of people the document flows to, notifications that need to be sent out, etc. by providing a configure workflow button 418. In some examples, the contract document can be set up to include webhooks to manage how a document is created and be shared with other applications, like a contract documents datastore of a same enterprise.\n[0049] It may be appreciated that in all of the above described processes, the clauses in the contact document being processed can be dragged and dropped, re-arranged as required in the document window 406 by the user. Further, as illustrated in FIG. 4, the user interface 400 may allow saving templates (as generated so far) by providing a save template button 420. It may be understood that when the save template button 420 is pushed, the fields identified in the contract document being processed are replaced by an identifiable string in the backend (for example, a party field may be replaced with a string like “#@#Party1#@#”). Now, the created (saved) contract document may be used as a template for generating other contract documents of the same types, as discussed in the proceeding paragraphs. In some examples, as illustrated in FIG. 4, the user interface 400 may further allow users to download a copy of the created contract document template, for reference and perusal of the user, by providing a download template button 422.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [9190, 5612, 551, 419, 4710, 58, 15, 15, 19, 16, 60, 8550, 14443, 311, 37599, 13, 220, 19, 11, 35662, 374, 264, 18239, 1196, 3749, 320, 300, 15251, 553, 5785, 83292, 220, 19, 15, 15, 8, 369, 6825, 264, 3811, 504, 458, 6350, 5116, 2197, 11, 304, 183