INFO 03-28 14:00:40 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-c557c714d15b4d3dbc07a3f8dd1dafe3-0: prompt: "Mark V. Pollio, Owner's Expert Report: Please accept this submission as my Report in the arbitration proceeding entitled Columbia Architects v. Warbucks Enterprises, LLC, AAA Case No. 2020-1040. I have been engaged by the attorneys for Respondent-Counterclaimant Warbucks to provide professional consulting and give expert testimony in this proceeding on a single question—did Claimants engage in professional malpractice in failing to incorporate a change in the floor-area ratio in the New York City Zoning Resolution in their architectural plans before construction of the project at issue? My answer to that question is yes: the failure to recognize the change resulted in the design and construction of an illegal extra floor, which has to be removed and has caused immense financial damage to Respondent through delays and extra expenses.\nQualifications\nI am an architect licensed in the States of New York, Connecticut, and North Dakota, as well as Italy, France, and the United Kingdom. I am a member of the American Institute of Architects, the Institute for Classical Architecture and Art, the Royal Society of British Architects, the Soane Society, the Society of Architectural Historians, the Italian Society of Architectural Technology (SITdA) and the Académie d’architecture. and hold a certification from the National Council of Architectural Registration Boards (NCARB). I have taught architectural technology at the Universities of Bologna, Padua, Paris 12 Val de Marne, and Imperial College London, and have lectured on aspects of architecture at New York University and the Georgia Institute of Technology. I write frequently on construction topics, including ten books on architecture, which have been issued in many editions and languages and are sometimes used as textbooks in architecture schools that care about architectural history and are not in thrall to the next new thing.\nIn the 1970s I designed several structures which were favorably reviewed and nominated for important awards. Since then, my commitment to architectural scholarship and writing have left me with little time to compete for commissions. However, I have been frequently retained as an expert consultant and witness. As of this writing I had appeared as a witness at deposition or in trial in 36 court cases and have consulted in over 100 mediations and have given testimony in 73 arbitrations. So frequent have my appearances in these matters been that one lawyer for an adverse party actually called me a professional expert witness! In every case I have been involved in, the tribunal has found me to be a qualified expert. Some tribunals have given more weight to my testimony than others, but all have acknowledged my qualifications.\nINFORMATION RELIED UPON\nMy experience as an expert witness has demonstrated that the credibility and accuracy of my opinions depend greatly on the extent and quality of the background information I have reviewed. To prepare for giving my opinion in this case, I have reviewed all the submissions of the parties to the American Arbitration Association, materials filed with the New York City Department of Buildings and the Board of Standards and Appeals, and relevant provisions of the NYC Building Code, including the Zoning Resolution provisions relevant to the Zoning District in which the structure in question has been erected.\nQUESTION PRESENTED AND OPINION\nAs stated in greater detail below, the building being developed by Respondent Warbucks was intended to be a 20-story mixed-use commercial and residential structure. Claimants’ architectural plans correctly reflected Respondent’s wishes and intent by providing for that height. The zoning regulations permitted this at the time the design work was going through the Schematic Design Phase of the project, but in 2017, while Design Development was being completed, the Zoning Resolution was modified in such a way that the floor-area ratio of the contemplated structure would no longer support a 20-story building. Only after the building had topped out did the building inspectors discern that the structure was a floor too high. A stop work order was issued, and the offending floor had to be removed.\nThe question I have been asked to give an opinion on is whether Claimant committed professional malpractice in not re-examining the relevant regulatory provisions before issuing their final construction documents and allowing the general contractor to erect the building’s superstructure. Another way of putting the question is whether Claimant’s conduct constituted a departure from the standard of professional care that must guide their work as architects in the State of New York. My opinion is YES — Claimant departed from the standard of care and accordingly committed professional malpractice.\nBASIS FOR OPINION\nIn arriving at my opinion, I assumed the facts stated below to be true. Respondent intended the Building to be 20-stories in height and so advised Claimants. The parties’ relationship is governed by an AIA B101-2007 Owner/Architect Agreement, dated as of May 1, 2016. The following relevant provisions appear in that contract:\n§ 3.1.1 The Architect shall manage the Architect’s services, consult with the Owner, research applicable design criteria, attend Project meetings, communicate with members of the Project team and report progress to the Owner.\n§ 3.1.5 The Architect shall, at appropriate times, contact the governmental authorities required to approve the Construction Documents and the entities providing utility services to the Project. In designing the Project, the Architect shall respond to applicable design requirements imposed by such governmental authorities and by such entities providing utility services.\nThese contractual terms required Claimants to “research applicable design criteria,” which included a duty to study the local regulations governing the size and dimensions of the structure they were designing for Respondent’s lot. The resulting drawings had to “respond to applicable design requirements imposed by such governmental authorities.” Thus, the architectural drawings were required to comply with any governmental limitations upon size and dimensions. It was incumbent upon Claimants to “contact the governmental authorities” to be sure they were complying with applicable regulations. The principal such authority here would have been the NYC Department of Buildings (DOB).\nAs it turns out, the DOB has contended that the height of the building designed by Claimants violates the floor-area ratio (FAR) mandated by Article IX, Chapter 1, § 91-21, of the Zoning Resolution. The FAR provides the formula that allows an architect to determine the allowable height of any building in the City of New York. No violation had occurred at the time Claimant w performing Schematic Design Phase work in 2016, but it appears that in 2017 the NYC Planning Commission recommended, and the City Council approved, a change in the Zoning Resolution that altered the FAR for the Zoning District in which Respondent’s building was being erected.\nIn 2018, after the superstructure had topped out at 20 stories, the DOB issued a stop-work order based on its contention that the building was too high and that construction could not resume unless and until the structure had been reduced in height by one story. Since then, the project has been in suspense while Respondent has engaged in an appeal process through the DOB and the New York City Board of Standards and Appeals. Two years of delay have set back Respondent’s efforts to complete this development.\nNeither the Architect nor Respondent picked up on the change in the FAR. Claimants’ failure to honor the terms of the B101 constitutes a major contributing cause both to the delay and to Respondent being unable to construct a building of the size and height it intended. The failure by the Architect to perform a follow-up review of the Building Code and related regulations constituted a departure from the standard of professional care. Every architect must be aware that building regulations change from time to time and cannot rest content that review of the regulations a year or more earlier will be sufficient at the time construction is about to begin.\nI do recognize that the general contractor engaged by Respondent also had a provision in its agreement requiring it to be familiar with and act pursuant to applicable building regulations and that, in its relations with others on the project, such as the Architect of Record, Respondent is to some degree responsible for negligent conduct by the contractors it has engaged. Nevertheless, the Architect runs the project from the standpoint of having responsibility for contract administration (§ 3.6.1) and is supposed to coordinate with Respondent’s consultants. I also recognize that Claimant seem to have some merit in their claim that they relied upon those communications in believing they could proceed using the FAR that was in effect when they began their Schematic Design. None of this, however, diminishes Claimants’ responsibility to be diligent in making a supplemental review of regulations before construction began.\nCOMPENSATION\nMy compensation for both consulting services and serving as an expert witness in connection with these proceedings is entirely time-based at a rate of $1,000 per hour. It has also been indicated that Respondent is involved in two other cases relating to the same subject matter, one in state court and the other before the NYC Board of Standards and Appeals, and if my performance in this case results in a favorable outcome for Respondent, it will engage me to serve as an expert in those cases as well.", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], s