 kind of my, this like a little bit of a recreation of a memory I had.\n\nSo I wouldn't say it's authentic at all, but it's just kind of like the flavors that I love. I'm adding 1/2 a teaspoon of crushed red pepper for some heat.\n\n 1/2 a teaspoon of salt. We can add more later if we need. And as an optional little addition, 1/2 a teaspoon of smoked paprika. You can add whatever spices you want. The soup is really flavorful and delicious, but it accepts spices beautifully, so add all your favorites in, go to town.\n\n You can also find a lot of different variations of the classic recipe. If you are going to Caribbean countries, you'll see different flavors added and so on and so forth. So it's really like a blank canvas that you can add so many things to. Okay, stir that in. It smells so good already.\n\nThis is like, hmm, this is what cooking should be. I'm gonna stir this for about two minutes, stirring constantly so the garlic doesn't burn but does become super fragrant. I'm also blooming those spices in the hot, hot oil and really letting their flavor kinda just explode out of there. Okay. Oops, I forgot one thing.\n\n I'm gonna turn this off.\n\nTake it off heat. We have to drain and rinse all of our black beans. So open those cans up, and let's rinse 'em out. If you have any good suggestions for that bean juice, or aquafaba, you can let me know in the comments.\n\n If these were garbanzo beans or chickpeas, I would totally save that. There's so many good uses for it. But I'm not sure about the black bean stuff. All right, my beans are clean. Back to business.\n\n Back to medium heat.\n\nI'm gonna add my black beans. Four cups of veggie broth. If you wanted to make this non-vegetarian, you could definitely use chicken broth, beef broth, any of your favorites. This smells delicious, but it would taste pretty dull, because we need some acid in here.\n\n I want about 1/4 cup of fresh lime juice. Squeeze that right in. Ah. All my might. I used to have a little lime press, remember?\n\n Cannot find my squeezer, so a fork will do just fine. Depending on how juicy your lime is in. (bell dings) Depending on how juicier your limes are, you might need a few for 1/4 cup. Or you could be lucky and get one giant juicy lime, mm. One suggestion, just don't use the bottled lime juice.\n\n Mm-mm, it's not the same. There are a bunch of soups that my mom used to make on repeat when I was little, and a lot of them were finished off with a drizzle of olive oil and a drizzle of vinegar. I feel like it's a really classic way to finish a soup. Gives you a little bit of richness and that nice acidity too.\n\nIf you did that too, let me know.\n\n I just wanna see if it's super common or kind of random. Stir that lime juice in. And now we're gonna bring this to a boil over medium high heat. The soup needs to come together, so we have to break down these flavors and let them infuse into the broth. My soup's come to a boil.\n\n I'm gonna reduce this heat to low, cover it, and let it simmer away.\n\nThe secret to most soups, but especially this one, is the simmer time. It's actually all the spices and flavorings, but the other secret is the simmer time to let those flavors come together. And there's a, even though most of the ingredients are here right now, there's a couple more steps to go just to get every bit of taste and texture outta these ingredients. But for now, we're gonna let the soup simmer away for 25 minutes.\n\n Just remember to stir occasionally. My soup's been bubbling away for a little bit over 25 minutes.\n\nSo now, ooh, so steamy, in the ladle, about two cups right into my blender. This tastes really nice already, but I want a velvety smooth consistency, so. Where's the cups?\n\n (chuckles) There we go, that's about two cups. Open the blender vent. This is so hot, it could just push the top off if the vent isn't open, so leave it open, but maybe just a little bit of coverage. Okay, we're gonna pulse this until it's nice and smooth. And then, we're just gonna pour this back in.\n\nThere we go. And you're gonna let this cook uncovered for 10 to 20 minutes, just so it thickens up even more. Super thick, you want it. (speaks in foreign language) If you want this super thick, closer to 20. More slack, 10 or less.\n\n Before you leave your soup to simmer, it's time for a little taste test. This is where you want to add in any additional seasonings. Hmm. I mean, I really like that, but if you wanted to add some more salt, pepper, lime, whatever else you want to add, this is the time so it has that 10 to 20 minutes just to blend in and release some more flavor. That's good.\n\n Serve topped with avocado, cilantro, and lime, and you're ready to enjoy. That is so good. It's just hearty, comforting. It has wonderful hits of acid and tang, and all the spices coming together with all that cook time. I hope you get a chance to make this recipe.\n\n And if you liked this video, check out my Latin playlist.\n\n(upbeat music).", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [12, 27553, 11, 358, 2776, 3757, 30563, 613, 11, 323, 3351, 389, 4968, 33963, 19354, 11, 582, 2299, 3259, 264, 3974, 323, 4135, 3691, 20427, 19174, 13, 2055, 1077, 594, 633, 3855, 13, 5512, 1007, 11, 11633, 264, 11051, 10043, 331, 23387, 476, 3338, 13, 1205, 2299, 16519, 912, 3040, 55488, 11, 476, 220, 16, 14, 19, 10525, 11, 315, 32113, 5590, 1290, 1119, 279, 3338, 13, 10771, 279, 3338, 916, 264, 11051, 8628, 382, 358, 1366, 847, 5590, 311, 387, 27966, 304, 279, 7215, 11, 773, 1393, 432, 594, 23867, 705, 11, 358, 2776, 16519,