INFO 03-28 14:02:18 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-85c734422038420dbeb22e533e14b478-0: prompt: 'Can you please find the most important quotes from this transcript and list them in bullet points with time stamps, along with which speaker said it: >> The former attorney general\n0:01\nwho resigned and broke\n0:03\nwith President Trump\n0:04\nthis week on "Firing Line."\n0:07\n>> Bill Barr can go down\n0:09\nas the greatest attorney general\n0:10\nin the history of our country\n0:12\nor he can go down\n0:13\nas just another guy.\n0:15\n>> "One Damn Thing After\n0:16\nAnother" is the title\n0:17\nof William Barr\'s memoir\n0:19\nand a fitting description\n0:20\nof his time\n0:21\nas Trump\'s Attorney General.\n0:23\nBarr was firmly in Trump\'s camp\n0:24\nwhen it came to denouncing\n0:25\nthe Russia probe...\n0:26\n>> I think that the government\n0:27\ndid not have\n0:28\na prosecutable case.\n0:30\n>> ...and warning about\n0:30\nthe possibility of voter fraud\n0:32\nbefore the 2020 election.\n0:34\n>> Elections that have been held\n0:36\nwith mail have found\n0:37\nsubstantial fraud and coercion.\n0:39\n>> But after Trump lost,\n0:43\nthey had a stunning break.\n0:44\n>> I made it clear\n0:45\nI did not agree\n0:46\nwith the idea of saying\n0:48\nthe election was stolen.\n0:49\n>> As the former president\n0:51\nlaunches a new White House bid,\n0:53\nand prosecutors\n0:54\nbuild cases against Trump\n0:55\nand his businesses,\n0:56\nwhat does former Attorney\n0:57\nGeneral William Barr say now?\n1:01\n>> "Firing Line\n1:02\nwith Margaret Hoover"\n1:03\nis made possible in part by...\n1:12\n...and by...\n1:23\nCorporate funding is provided\n1:25\nby...\n1:29\n>> Attorney General\n1:30\nWilliam Barr,\n1:30\nwelcome to "Firing Line."\n1:32\n>> Thank you, Margaret.\n1:33\nGreat to be here.\n1:34\n>> You were the attorney general\n1:35\nfor two presidents\n1:37\nin two different centuries --\n1:39\nPresident George H.W. Bush and\n1:40\nPresident Donald Trump.\n1:42\nYou resigned after the 2020\n1:44\nelection, when you broke\n1:46\nfrom President Trump over\n1:47\nhis 2020 election fraud claims.\n1:50\nThis week, Donald Trump\n1:51\nannounced that he will seek\n1:53\nthe Republican nomination again\n1:55\nto be president.\n1:56\nAnd I wonder,\n1:57\nwhat was your reaction?\n2:00\n>> Well, I made it clear\n2:03\nin my book, over a year ago,\n2:06\nthat I didn\'t think he was\n2:07\nthe right person to lead\n2:08\neither the Republican Party\n2:10\nor the country forward.\n2:12\nAnd I would hope\n2:13\nthat he stepped down.\n2:14\nSo I was disappointed\n2:15\nthat he announced.\n2:17\n>> He\'s facing unprecedented\n2:19\nfederal, state, and\n2:20\ncongressional investigations.\n2:23\nDo you think he\'s running\n2:23\nto insulate himself\n2:25\nfrom the investigations?\n2:28\n>> I think that could be\n2:28\npart of the reason.\n2:30\nBut I think he, you know,\n2:31\nhe has a monumental ego,\n2:34\nand he doesn\'t want to go down\n2:36\nin history as a loser.\n2:38\nAnd he also would like\n2:40\nto get revenge on the people\n2:41\nthat he feels\n2:42\ndidn\'t give him his due.\n2:45\n>> You said he doesn\'t want\n2:47\nto be seen as a loser.\n2:48\nBut the 2022 midterm elections\n2:51\nhad him on the ballot\n2:53\nin absentia in many, many cases.\n2:55\nAnd another big loser\n2:57\nof the midterms was this lie\n2:59\nabout the "stop the steal."\n3:02\nAnd in the case where\n3:02\nstop-the-steal candidates\n3:04\nwere on the ballot,\n3:04\nthey overwhelmingly lost.\n3:06\n>> Right.\n3:07\n>> His hand-picked candidates\n3:09\noverwhelmingly lost.\n3:12\nHow do you reflect on\n3:13\nthe midterms and the fact\n3:14\nthat the election fraud\n3:15\nthat you resigned over\n3:18\nbecame a losing political point\n3:20\nfor the GOP?\n3:22\n>> Well, again, it reflects\n3:24\nTrump\'s poor judgment\n3:25\nand his ego.\n3:26\nLook at Ronald Reagan in 1976.\n3:30\nOkay?\n3:30\nHe spent four years building the\n3:33\nunity of the Republican Party.\n3:35\nHe even discouraged\n3:37\nhis conservative supporters\n3:38\nwho had backed him,\n3:39\nmany of them, from challenging\n3:41\nsome of the so-called\n3:42\n"establishment Republicans"\n3:43\nbecause he wanted to unify\n3:45\nthe party.\n3:47\nAnd Trump has done\n3:48\njust the opposite,\n3:49\nfor no purpose other than\n3:50\nhis ego gratification.\n3:53\nAnd he picked candidates\n3:55\nthat were weak candidates\n3:56\nsimply because they were willing\n3:58\nto show their fealty\n3:59\nto him by saying\n4:01\nthat the election was stolen.\n4:02\nAnd that backfired completely.\n4:05\n>> You\'re clear in your book\n4:06\nthe GOP needs to move on\n4:07\nfrom Donald Trump.\n4:07\n>> Yeah.\n4:08\n>> Does it concern you there\n4:09\ncould be a splintered field,\n4:10\nand that Trump could come away\n4:12\nwith the plurality, the largest\n4:13\nshare of the primary base vote?\n4:15\n>> Right.\n4:16\nI don\'t think the field is going\n4:17\nto be as splintered\n4:18\nas it was in 2016,\n4:20\nwhich obviously helped Trump.\n4:23\nI also don\'t think Trump\n4:24\nwill be as strong.\n4:25\nI think he has been fading\n4:27\nfor months now.\n4:28\nAnd the recent\n4:31\nmidterm elections, I think,\n4:32\nhave really popped his balloon.\n4:34\n>> If you were\n4:35\nin Trump\'s ear now\n4:36\nand he were listening to you --\n4:38\nbig hypothetical --\n4:39\nwhat would you tell him?\n4:43\n>> I would him them essentially\n4:44\nwhat I told him all the way\n4:45\nthrough the administration,\n4:47\nwhich is that if he would\n4:49\njust -- if he had just exerted\n4:53\nsome self-control and\n4:54\ndiscipline, and dialed back his,\n4:58\nyou know, pugnacity\n5:01\nand his nastiness a little bit,\n5:03\nhe would have won the presidency\n5:04\nand had a second term.\n5:06\nAnd he didn\'t do that.\n5:08\nHe failed.\n5:09\nHe didn\'t do what\n5:10\nthe whole country hoped,\n5:13\nthat he would rise to the\n5:14\noccasion and rise to the office.\n5:15\nAnd he didn\'t do that.\n5:17\nSo he\'s had his chance.\n5:18\nHe doesn\'t -- He obviously\n5:20\ndoes not have the qualities\n5:21\nnecessary to unite the party,\n5:22\nwhich is the first step\n5:25\non the road back.\n5:27\nAnd he should stand aside.\n5:31\n>> The two presidents you served\n5:32\napproached DOJ differently.\n5:34\nAnd they approached you as\n5:35\nattorney general differently\n5:36\nfrom the position of the\n5:37\nWhite House.\n5:37\nCan you describe that?\n5:40\n>> Well, first, after Watergate,\n5:41\nRepublicans generally\n5:44\ntook the position that\n5:45\nthe Department of Justice\n5:46\nshould not be tampered with.\n5:48\nAnd that was Bush\'s philosophy.\n5:52\nHe followed my advice.\n5:53\nHe didn\'t, you know,\n5:54\ntry to cut corners\n5:55\nor get around the advice.\n5:57\nAnd he was very\n5:59\nrespectful of the role played\n6:00\nby the Department of Justice.\n6:02\nObviously,\n6:03\nTrump was a different animal.\n6:06\n>> Would you say Trump was\n6:07\nas respectful of the department\n6:08\nas President H.W. Bush was?\n6:10\n>> No, I think he viewed it as,\n6:13\nyou know, like any agency,\n6:15\nsomething that was under\n6:16\nhis command and could, you know,\n6:18\nhe could sort of potentially use\n6:21\nas a political tool.\n6:23\n>> You write in your memoir...\n6:39\n...and that Trump agreed not to\n6:42\ninterfere with your decisions.\n6:43\n>> That\'s right.\n6:44\n>> How\'d that go?\n6:45\n>> Well, he didn\'t interfere\n6:47\nwith them in the sense\n6:47\nof telling me, you know --\n6:49\n>> Directly.\n6:50\n>> Directly.\n6:50\nBut what -- You know,\n6:52\nhe would tweet.\n6:53\n>> Yeah.\n6:53\n>> So, for example, you know, he\n6:56\nwould tweet that he, you know,\n6:57\nhe wanted Comey indicted,\n6:59\nor he wanted McCabe\n7:00\nindicted, and...\n7:02\n>> James Clapper, John Brennan.\n7:04\n>> When we got into 2020,\n7:07\nyou know, he started tweeting\n7:08\nthat, you know, "I hope\n7:09\nBill Barr, you know, has the\n7:12\nguts to indict or, you know,\n7:14\ngo after Biden and Obama."\n7:16\n>> Materially, is there\n7:17\nany difference between him\n7:18\npressuring you privately\n7:19\nor doing it publicly?\n7:24\nI mean, I don\'t know which is\n7:25\nworse.\n7:25\n>> Well, that\'s why I said,\n7:26\nyou know,\n7:27\nhis behavior was different,\n7:28\nand that\'s why I asked him\n7:29\nto stop tweeting.\n7:30\nAnd for a couple --\n7:33\nHe took a few parting shots\n7:34\nto show who was in charge,\n7:35\nand then he quieted down\n7:36\nfor a couple of months.\n7:37\nBut in May, he just went --\n7:39\n>> Went back up again.\n7:40\n>> Yeah. Boom.\n7:41\n>> You\'ve noted George H.W.\n7:42\nBush, when he was president\n7:44\nand you were attorney general,\n7:44\nyou only met with him\n7:45\na couple of times.\n7:47\n>> A few times.\n7:47\n>> On the flip side --\n7:48\n>> No, one-on-one.\n7:49\n>> One-on-one, right.\n7:51\nBut on the flip side,\n7:51\nwith Trump, you characterized\n7:54\nthat you know, you often\n7:55\nspoke multiple times a day.\n7:57\nWas that because the presidents\n7:58\nhad different views\n7:58\nof the Justice Department,\n7:59\nas you outlined earlier?\n8:02\n>> Well, it wasn\'t just\n8:05\nthe Department of Justice.\n8:06\nIt was his way of managing\n8:08\nthe executive branch,\n8:09\nand his way, really even\n8:11\nbefore he became president,\n8:12\nhe would reach out\n8:13\nand talk to a lot of people,\n8:15\nand sort of take\n8:15\nall the information in\n8:17\nand test his own positions\n8:19\nand ideas with other people.\n8:22\nAnd so the whole\n8:24\nrunning of the administration\n8:26\nhad this sort of free --\n8:27\nI compared it to a card game\n8:30\nin my fraternity\n8:31\nwhen I was in college,\n8:32\nwhich is somehow\n8:33\nas the card game kept on going,\n8:35\nbut the cast of characters\n8:36\nchanged almost imperceptibly,\n8:38\nand you can never tell\n8:38\nwhen something started\n8:39\nand when something began.\n8:41\nAnd that\'s how the Oval Office\n8:42\nwas, you know, people coming in\n8:43\nand, "Hey, Bill, come on in."\n8:46\nSome meeting on trade.\n8:47\nI was called into some meeting\n8:48\non trade.\n8:49\nI didn\'t know\n8:50\nanything about the topic.\n8:51\n"What do you think about that?"\n8:52\nI mean, that\'s the way he is.\n8:53\nNow, there\'s a good side to\n8:54\nthat.\n8:54\nYou know, it allows\n8:56\nfor internal debate.\n8:57\nIt allows the Cabinet secretary\n9:01\nto have his day in court\n9:02\nwith the president.\n9:04\nAnd, you know, it adds a certain\n9:07\ndynamism, you know,\n9:09\nbut it also ignores\n9:11\nprocess and so --\n9:13\n>> And processes\n9:13\nare there for a reason.\n9:14\n>> Yeah, the processes are there\n9:15\nfor a reason.\n9:16\nSometimes I took advantage\n9:17\nof the free flow of things\n9:20\nto advance what I thought\n9:22\nwas the right thing to do.\n9:23\nAnd other cabinets sometimes\n9:24\nkneecapped me, you know,\n9:26\nbecause they were advancing\n9:27\nwhat they wanted to do.\n9:28\n>> Chaos benefits everyone,\n9:29\nyeah.\n9:29\n>> Chaos might be --\n9:30\nThere was chaos.\n9:31\n>> In 1987, "Firing Line"\n9:33\nhosted a debate\n9:35\nwith the Republican presidential\n9:36\ncandidates.\n9:38\nAnd your former boss\n9:39\nGeorge H.W. Bush was\n9:41\none of the participants.\n9:42\nAnd he was asked why Republicans\n9:44\nwere running on a platform\n9:46\nthat government,\n9:47\nto paraphrase Ronald Reagan,\n9:49\nwas the problem,\n9:50\nnot the solution.\n9:52\nTake a look at his answer.\n9:54\n>> I\'m not anti-government.\n9:57\nWhen I was at the CIA I ran into\n10:00\nsome of the finest,\n10:02\nmost public-spirited people\n10:03\nI\'ve ever served with.\n10:05\nAnd so I\'m not one who tears\n10:06\ndown those who have elected\n10:08\nto serve their country.\n10:10\nBut really, I think what it\n10:11\nboils down to is leadership.\n10:13\nLeadership in the White House\n10:15\nand leadership\n10:16\nin the United States Congress.\n10:17\nDon\'t blame those\n10:18\nthat make a lifetime\n10:19\nof service to the government.\n10:21\nGive them the kind of leadership\n10:22\nthey need and they\'ll follow\n10:24\nand get the job done.\n10:26\n>> You worked under\n10:27\nGeorge H.W. Bush when he was CIA\n10:29\ndirector as well,\n10:30\nwhen you were still at the CIA.\n10:32\nAnd you write in your book...\n10:44\nTalk more about what you learned\n10:45\nfrom Bush\'s leadership.\n10:47\n>> Well, exactly that.\n10:49\nHe was CIA director for one year\n10:52\nand that institution\n10:54\nis now named after him.\n10:55\nWhen you drive by it in Langley,\n10:57\nit\'s the George H.W. Bush\n10:59\nCenter.\n11:01\nAnd he was highly regarded\n11:04\nbecause he basically\n11:07\ntrusted people in the agency.\n11:09\nHe didn\'t separate himself\n11:12\nfrom them and, you know,\n11:13\nbash them and so forth.\n11:15\nNow, I have to say\n11:17\nthat it was a different age.\n11:20\nThings have, you know, evolved.\n11:22\nSo, you know, I wouldn\'t take\n11:26\nwhat he said to say,\n11:28\n"Well, gee, you know, the people\n11:29\nwho are saying\n11:30\nthere\'s a deep state today\n11:31\nare, you know, are wrong,"\n11:33\nright?\n11:34\nThere is a deep state.\n11:35\n>> That\'s where I --\n11:36\nYou read my mind.\n11:37\nThat\'s where I\'m going.\n11:38\nSo given that you\'ve worked\n11:39\nwith career professionals,\n11:41\nyou have had a long career of\n11:43\ninterfacing with civil servants\n11:45\nwho have made their life\n11:46\nin the government.\n11:48\nI think you\'re actually\n11:49\nparticularly well-suited\n11:50\nto comment about the state\n11:52\nof the deep state -- whether\n11:54\nit exists, how it exists,\n11:55\nwhat it looks like, to put some\n11:56\nnuance and some texture\n11:58\naround it for me.\n12:00\n>> Well, I think the people\n12:00\nwho attack the deep state --\n12:02\nand I am critical of the deep\n12:04\nstate and believe it exists --\n12:05\nbut there\'s also\n12:06\nsome of the rhetoric\n12:07\nand some of the positioning --\n12:08\n>> So how do you define it?\n12:09\n>> The way I would define\n12:10\nthe deep state\n12:11\nis an increased willingness\n12:13\nby more and more government\n12:16\ncivil servants\n12:18\nto pursue political objectives\n12:21\nrather than stand up\n12:22\nfor the values of the\n12:24\ninstitution they\'re a part of.\n12:25\nThey are not neutral.\n12:27\nThey\'re not politically neutral.\n12:29\nBut on the other side\n12:30\nof the ledger, okay,\n12:32\nis that I think\n12:34\nthere\'s an exaggeration\n12:36\nof its pervasiveness.\n12:38\nIt\'s bad.\n12:39\nIt does pervert government.\n12:42\nBut I still think the vast\n12:44\nmajority of civil servants\n12:45\ntry to do an honest job\n12:47\nand try to check their politics\n12:48\nat the door.\n12:49\nAnd I think that President\n12:52\nBush\'s quote that you played\n12:54\nis right on the money.\n12:56\nThe most important thing\n12:56\nis leadership.\n12:58\nWhen you provide clear guidance\n13:00\nand goals, people will --\n13:02\nthe institution\n13:03\nwill deliver for you.\n13:04\nYou can move the institution.\n13:07\nAnd I think people who come in\n13:11\nto run the government\n13:12\nand try to govern,\n13:14\nwho treat government employees\n13:16\nas pariahs,\n13:18\nare not doing their job.\n13:21\n>> Let me ask you\n13:22\nabout something\n13:22\nyou referred to earlier.\n13:25\nYou talked about --\n13:26\nYou were referring to President\n13:27\nTrump\'s tweeting\n13:29\nand sort of open, I mean,\n13:32\nI would call it sort of open\n13:33\nbullying about\n13:34\nwhat the Department of Justice\n13:35\nshould be doing.\n13:37\nTrump publicly expressed\n13:39\ndisapproval for how Justice\n13:41\nhandled Michael Flynn\n13:43\nand Roger Stone.\n13:45\nAnd under your tenure,\n13:47\nthe Department of Justice\n13:48\ndropped the prosecution\n13:49\nagainst General Flynn,\n13:51\nand then, in the case of Stone,\n13:52\noverruled prosecutors\n13:53\nby lowering the recommended\n13:54\nsentencing of prison time.\n13:56\nWhat do you say to the critics\n13:58\nwho point that DOJ charged\n14:01\napproximately 80,000 cases\n14:04\nin your tenure, per year,\n14:06\nand that only two got set aside,\n14:11\nthat happened to be the ones\n14:12\nthat President Trump\n14:14\nwas most audible on?\n14:17\n>> In the Department of Justice,\n14:19\ntough decisions are elevated\n14:21\nor where there\'s a disagreement\n14:23\nover a decision, it\'s elevated\n14:24\nand eventually it finds its way\n14:26\nto the attorney general\n14:27\nwho has to make a decision.\n14:29\nAnd in both of those cases,\n14:30\nthere were events that brought\n14:33\nthose matters to my desk.\n14:35\nAnd as I explain in my book\n14:37\nand I go through all the details\n14:38\nof both episodes,\n14:40\nwhat I tried to do in every case\n14:42\nwas do what I thought\n14:43\nwas right under our standards\n14:44\nof the Department of Justice\n14:46\nand even-handed justice,\n14:47\nand I couldn\'t allow the fact\n14:50\nthat the president was tweeting\n14:51\nabout stuff\n14:52\nprevent me from doing that.\n14:56\n>> So knowing that the optics\n14:57\nrisked looking as though\n14:58\nit was political,\n15:01\nyou went ahead and --\n15:02\n>> That\'s the attorney general\'s\n15:03\njob.\n15:04\nIf the attorney general starts\n15:05\nsaying,\n15:06\nyou know, how is this going --\n15:06\nyou know, "Am I going to be\n15:07\nattacked if I do this?"\n15:09\nand then doesn\'t do\n15:10\nwhat you think is right,\n15:11\nthat\'s not the kind of attorney\n15:12\ngeneral you want.\n15:13\nYou want an attorney general\n15:14\nwho will try to do the right\n15:16\nthing regardless of the cost\n15:17\nto themselves.\n15:18\n>> How important is character...\n15:20\n>> In a president?\n15:21\n>> ...in a president,\n15:22\nfrom your position as having\n15:23\nbeen attorney general twice?\n15:26\n>> Well, I think, you know,\n15:27\nI think it\'s\n15:28\nextremely important.\n15:30\nThe character is very important.\n15:32\nBut when we live in an age\n15:34\nwhere the political differences\n15:35\nare so polarized\n15:38\nand the swings between\n15:40\nsort of almost socialism\n15:42\nand more conservative\n15:44\nRepublican policies,\n15:45\nand there\'s a broad swing,\n15:47\nI think you have to look at that\n15:50\njust as importantly.\n15:51\nSo, for example,\n15:53\na progressive Democrat,\n15:54\nas an individual,\n15:56\nmight have a lot of character,\n15:58\nmore than\n15:58\nthe Republican candidate.\n16:00\nI would not cast my vote\n16:01\njust based on their character,\n16:03\nI would also -- I would look at\n16:05\nthe impact on the United States\n16:07\nand what it means\n16:07\nfor the country.\n16:08\n>> But character\n16:09\nis more important than ideology?\n16:11\nOr as important as ideology?\n16:12\n>> As important.\n16:14\nNowadays, yes.\n16:17\n>> Really?\n16:19\n>> Well, think about it.\n16:22\nYou know, we\'re facing a very\n16:26\ndangerous world right now.\n16:28\nI think as a voter\n16:29\nyou should say,\n16:31\n"What, you know, is the impact\n16:32\non the country of this person\n16:34\nleading the country\n16:35\nfor the next four years?"\n16:39\nSome of our great presidents\n16:41\nhave not been moral exemplars.\n16:44\n>> No, I think --\n16:47\nI think the question\n16:48\nof character is important.\n16:49\nBut then it\'s also a question\n16:50\nof, in that character,\n16:53\nare you going to be faithful to\n16:54\nthe laws and the Constitution?\n16:56\n>> Well, I mean,\n16:57\nwhen we talk about character,\n16:58\nI was thinking broadly.\n16:59\nBut obviously it\'s very\n17:00\nimportant that a president --\n17:01\nYou know, the president is in\n17:03\ncharge of the executive branch,\n17:06\nand he is the top\n17:07\nlaw enforcement official.\n17:09\nBut what\'s critical\n17:10\nfor our country\n17:11\nand for our system of government\n17:13\nis that the law be applied\n17:14\nequally, especially in\n17:15\nthe criminal justice system.\n17:17\nAnd the president has to respect\n17:19\nthat, that you cannot use\n17:20\nthe criminal justice system\n17:22\nas a political weapon.\n17:23\n>> You broke with Trump\n17:24\nover the election,\n17:25\nas we\'ve discussed.\n17:28\nYou broke with him\n17:28\nover election fraud.\n17:30\nIn the lead-up to the election,\n17:32\nyou had been pointing\n17:34\nto election fraud\n17:35\nas a potential problem.\n17:37\nAnd looking back on it\n17:40\nand how how it turned out,\n17:42\nwhere there were\n17:43\nso many Americans\n17:44\nthat believed Donald Trump\n17:46\nand believed his election\n17:47\nfraud lies --\n17:48\neven after the attorney general\n17:49\nsaid there wasn\'t enough\n17:52\nelection fraud to have changed\n17:54\nthe outcome of the election --\n17:55\ndo you think back at the times\n17:58\nwhere you were raising\n17:59\nthe red flag prior to that\n18:02\nand consider whether that helped\n18:06\nlay the ground for people\n18:08\nto distrust\n18:10\nthe integrity of the elections\n18:11\nin the first place?\n18:13\n>> See, on the contrary,\n18:14\nI think what did was\n18:15\nthe irresponsible behavior\n18:17\nof the Democrats\n18:19\nwho, putting aside\n18:21\nthe question of fraud,\n18:22\nwere clearly monkeying\n18:24\nwith the rules and using COVID\n18:26\nas an excuse to do it,\n18:28\nto tilt the playing field\n18:29\nin ways that were unfair\n18:30\nto the Republicans\n18:31\nor which favored them.\n18:33\nAnd people saw that kind of\n18:34\ngamesmanship going on,\n18:36\nand that created the climate\n18:39\nin which they did not\n18:41\nhave trust in the election.\n18:42\nYou have, you know,\n18:44\npeople know what the mail system\n18:45\nis in this country.\n18:47\nPeople have had experience\n18:48\nwith it.\n18:49\nAnd they don\'t trust --\n18:50\nThey didn\'t trust the integrity\n18:51\nof the system.\n18:52\n>> Well, to the contrary,\n18:53\nthere are four states\n18:54\nthat had 100% mail-in voting.\n18:55\nI\'m from one of them, Colorado.\n18:56\n>> Right.\n18:57\n>> People have a really\n18:58\nhigh degree of faith\n18:59\nin the electorate, election\n19:01\nintegrity in those states.\n19:01\n>> Well, it depends\n19:02\non what kind of system you have.\n19:03\nIf you have a system where you\n19:04\nfill out applications\n19:07\nto get the ballot to mail in,\n19:09\nthat is a pretty\n19:10\ngood security system.\n19:11\nAnd the states that have done\n19:12\nthat are not a problem.\n19:13\nI said that publicly.\n19:15\nOkay?\n19:15\nWhat I objected to was universal\n19:18\nmailing out of ballots.\n19:21\nI was arguing against the --\n19:23\nwhat I considered to be\n19:24\nthe irresponsible,\n19:26\nreally incendiary\n19:28\ntactics of the Democrats,\n19:29\nwhich is essentially\n19:29\ntry to run elections\n19:30\non an honor system.\n19:32\nAnd I\'m saying you do that\n19:34\nand no one\'s going to trust\n19:35\nthe results, period.\n19:36\nAnd to raise that red flag\n19:38\nwas the right thing to do\n19:40\nto try to stop that kind of\n19:41\nirresponsible behavior.\n19:44\n>> You left the\n19:45\nTrump White House\n19:45\nin mid-December just after\n19:48\nthe Electoral College had voted.\n19:49\nAnd you wrote in your book...\n19:51\n"without minimizing both the\n19:53\nstupidity and shamefulness of\n19:55\nwhat happened" -- you were\n19:57\nreferring to January 6th --\n19:59\n"at the time I did not think the\n20:02\nrepublic was in genuine danger."\n20:04\n>> You know,\n20:04\nI don\'t really think so.\n20:05\nI think it\'s\n20:06\na little overdramatic.\n20:07\nYou know, I didn\'t see it as\n20:09\nsomething that was threatening a\n20:11\ncoup at the time.\n20:13\nIt was the way things operate\n20:14\nwhen Trump is directly\n20:15\nin charge of something\n20:17\nand they\'re not people who know\n20:18\nwhat they\'re doing involved.\n20:20\nIt was a mess.\n20:21\nIt was a Keystone Cops\n20:24\noperation.\n20:25\n>> Well, now I\'m thinking about\n20:26\nthe riot at the Capitol, though,\n20:27\nand the fact that violence\n20:28\nactually impeded the peaceful\n20:30\ntransition of power for a time.\n20:34\n>> Well, I mean,\n20:34\nthere was a delay.\n20:35\n>> Yeah.\n20:36\n>> Yeah.\n20:37\nBut I didn\'t think, you know,\n20:38\nthat, you know, our country\n20:40\nwas going to fall apart\n20:41\nbecause of that.\n20:42\n>> Or that the republic\n20:43\nwas in danger?\n20:44\n>> Or that, you know,\n20:44\nthere was going to be a coup.\n20:46\nAnd I\'ve said, look,\n20:46\nif there\'s evidence\n20:47\nthat people in the government,\n20:50\nthe White House, the president\n20:51\nor whatever were part\n20:53\nof a scheme to use violence\n20:54\nto delay the count\n20:56\nthat that would be a crime\n20:58\nand people should go to jail\n20:59\nfor that if it can be proved.\n21:01\nI haven\'t seen the evidence yet.\n21:03\nThere may have been\n21:04\nsome of the demonstrators\n21:05\nwho had that plan and they\n21:07\nhave been indicted for that.\n21:09\n>> But it was more than chaos.\n21:10\nI mean, six people died.\n21:12\n>> That\'s chaos.\n21:12\n>> And there\'s a real question\n21:14\nabout had any member of Congress\n21:16\nor Mike Pence come across\n21:18\nany of those rioters,\n21:20\nwhether they also might have\n21:21\nbeen violently attacked.\n21:23\n>> Yeah, I just --\n21:24\nI feel it is an exaggeration,\n21:26\na little bit of melodrama,\n21:28\nto treat this as the virtual or,\n21:31\nyou know, almost the collapse\n21:33\nof the American government\n21:34\nor the overthrow\n21:35\nof the government.\n21:36\nI thought it was a stupid,\n21:39\nreckless behavior\n21:42\nby the people involved.\n21:44\nCertainly the president,\n21:46\nI think, precipitated it.\n21:47\nWhether he legally incited\n21:49\nor not,\n21:49\nhe precipitated the whole thing.\n21:51\nAnd, you know, I let it be known\n21:55\nwhat my views were on that.\n21:56\n>> Yeah.\n21:57\nIn September, you said\n21:58\nthe Justice Department\n22:00\nwas getting close.\n22:01\nYou said, "getting close\n22:03\nto having enough evidence\n22:04\nto indict Trump\n22:05\nin the classified documents\n22:06\ninvestigation."\n22:08\nBut you also said you hope\n22:09\nthey don\'t indict him.\n22:12\nIn your view, is there ever\n22:13\na circumstance where you think\n22:15\nit is appropriate to indict\n22:17\na former president?\n22:19\n>> Oh, yes.\n22:23\nYou know, if a former president\n22:25\ncommits a crime, you know,\n22:26\nespecially a serious crime,\n22:28\nthey should be indicted for it.\n22:31\nIf the Department of Justice\n22:34\ncan show that these were indeed\n22:35\nvery sensitive documents,\n22:36\nwhich I think\n22:37\nthey probably were,\n22:39\nand also show that the president\n22:41\nconsciously was involved\n22:42\nin misleading the Department,\n22:45\ndeceiving the government,\n22:47\nand playing games after\n22:48\nhe had received the subpoena\n22:50\nfor the documents, that\'s --\n22:52\nthose are serious charges.\n22:54\n>> That\'s a serious\n22:55\nenough crime?\n22:55\n>> Well, I\'ve said that\n22:57\nI personally think\n22:58\nthat they probably have\n22:59\nthe basis for legitimately\n23:01\nindicting the president.\n23:01\nI don\'t know, I\'m speculating.\n23:03\nBut given what\'s gone on,\n23:05\nI think\n23:05\nthey probably have the evidence\n23:07\nthat would check the box.\n23:09\nThey have the case.\n23:10\n>> And if they have it,\n23:11\nshould they?\n23:13\n>> That\'s a decision for --\n23:15\n>> If you were A.G., would you?\n23:18\n>> I\'m not going to\n23:18\nget into that.\n23:20\n>> Do you think they will?\n23:22\n>> I think it\'s becoming\n23:23\nincreasingly more likely.\n23:25\n>> And you think it would\n23:25\nbe appropriate if they did?\n23:27\n>> Well, this is what the\n23:28\nattorney general gets paid --\n23:29\nthese kinds of decisions.\n23:31\nYou know, the argument for doing\n23:32\nit is that if you let someone\n23:34\nlike this who, you know --\n23:36\nif the facts are as raw\n23:37\nas they may be,\n23:39\nand you let someone get away\n23:40\nwith it, how can you\n23:42\nprotect these secrets?\n23:43\nHow can you insist on people\n23:45\nin government taking this stuff\n23:46\nseriously?\n23:49\nSo, you know, that\'s\n23:50\nan important thing to weigh,\n23:52\nas well as what the, you know,\n23:54\nwhat it will do to the country\n23:55\nand to the office\n23:56\nof the presidency.\n23:57\nAnd I think Merrick Garland is\n24:00\ngoing to have to make that call.\n24:02\n>> If Donald Trump\n24:03\nwere the nominee\n24:04\nfor the Republican Party again,\n24:05\nwould you support him?\n24:07\n>> Well, I\'m just hoping\n24:08\nit never comes to that\n24:09\nbecause I think it would be\n24:10\na tragedy if he\'s our nominee,\n24:11\nif he\'s the Republican nominee.\n24:14\n>> Could you vote against him?\n24:16\nCould you vote not for\n24:17\nthe Republican, if Donald Trump\n24:19\nwere the Republican nominee?\n24:20\n>> Well, again, I think\n24:20\nit gets down to what I said,\n24:22\nwhich is I would have to make\n24:23\nthe judgment at that point,\n24:25\nthe impact on the country.\n24:26\nAnd I\'ll have to see what\'s\n24:27\ngoing on in the world,\n24:29\nwho the Democratic nominee is.\n24:31\nIf it\'s a progressive\n24:32\nDemocratic nominee,\n24:33\nI can\'t imagine voting for them.\n24:35\n>> But ideologically progressive\n24:36\nversus somebody who orchestrated\n24:38\nan attack on the Capitol,\n24:39\nwho betrayed\n24:39\nhis oath to the Constitution,\n24:40\nas you said, in your words,\n24:42\nyou still might vote for him?\n24:44\n>> Mm-hmm.\n24:45\nDepending on all this --\n24:46\nIt\'s hard for me to project\n24:47\nwhat the future holds.\n24:49\nThe question is always\n24:50\na comparative one.\n24:51\nYou have to make a choice\n24:52\nbetween two people.\n24:53\nI don\'t believe in throwing\n24:54\nmy vote away on a third party.\n24:55\n>> So you don\'t rule it out?\n24:56\nYou don\'t rule out supporting\n24:57\nPresident Trump again?\n24:58\n>> No, I don\'t.\n24:59\nIt depends on the circumstances.\n25:01\nI pray that that doesn\'t come --\n25:04\nI think the reason our republic\n25:06\nis in jeopardy is because\n25:08\nwe are not throwing up\n25:10\nthe best and the brightest\n25:11\nfor these offices.\n25:12\nWe\'re not giving the American\n25:13\npeople good choices.\n25:14\nToo frequently, they\'re picking\n25:16\nthe lesser of two evils.\n25:18\nAnd we can\'t last very long\n25:21\nif that continues to happen.\n25:24\n>> Attorney General\n25:25\nWilliam Barr, thank you\n25:26\nfor joining me on "Firing Line."\n25:28\n>> Okay. Thanks.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [6713, 498, 4486, 1477, 279, 1429, 2989, 17194, 504, 419, 35715, 323, 1140, 1105, 304, 17432, 3501, 448, 882, 49212, 11, 3156, 448, 892, 18601, 1053, 432, 25, 3578, 576, 4741, 13747, 4586, 198, 15, 25, 15, 16, 198, 14623, 39107, 323, 14422, 198, 15, 25, 15, 18, 198, 4197, 4795, 3339, 198, 15, 25, 15, 19, 198, 574, 2003, 389, 330, 37, 6191, 7083, 10040, 15, 25, 15, 22, 198, 2452, 8596, 31717, 646, 728, 1495, 198, 15, 25, 15, 24, 198, 300, 279, 12196, 13747, 4586, 198, 15, 25, 16, 15, 198, 258, 279, 3840, 315, 1039, 3146, 198, 15, 25, 16, 17, 198, 269, 566, 646, 728, 1495, 198, 15, 25, 16, 18, 198, 300, 1101, 2441, 7412, 624, 15, 25, 16, 20, 198, 2452, 330, 3966, 82415, 37200, 4636, 198, 15, 25, 16, 21, 198, 14037, 1, 374, 279, 2265, 198, 15, 25, 16, 22, 198, 1055, 12375, 31717, 594, 50242, 198, 15, 25, 16, 24, 198, 437, 264, 26345, 4008, 198, 15, 25, 17, 15, 198, 1055, 806, 882, 198, 15, 25, 17, 16, 198, 300, 3339, 594, 18412, 3251, 624, 15, 25, 17, 18, 198, 33, 1118, 5