INFO 03-28 14:02:26 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-6dd06b753d814a45b32e2197bbc4c058-0: prompt: 'Write a script out of this for YouTube and audience men ages 18-49: Sam Harris is known as one of the most prominent critics of religion, and is a leading figure in the New Atheist movement. Harris is particularly opposed to what he refers to as dogmatic belief, and says that "Pretending to know things one doesn\'t know is a betrayal of science – and yet it is the lifeblood of religion."\n\nHow can you know something and what impact does that have on your ordinary life?\n\nFirst and foremost about scientific truth. He thinks \nMost important scientific truth there is\nFundamentally different kind of universe we live in.\nThere either is or there isn’t.\nIntelligence is something that comes late in the universe as a concept late in the universe. Material substrate latent potential asnd action of forming process on top of that. Intuitive apprehensive of the relationship between consciousness and living forms. \n\nThere is a spirit that gives rise to creation. \nComputation is algorithmic\nHe thinks thought is algorithmic\nGadder’s theorem is something is standing outside. \n\nConsciousness is implicit in matter; is non-sensical; according to Dawkins.\nMatter can give rise to consciousness with sufficient complexity say’s Dawkins.\nConsciousness shapes biological being.\n\nThe reason I ask this question is because the existence of God has major implications on how you live your life. For just a moment forget how you know that God exists or if you do not believe that God exists the forge judgment for just a moment. Consider, not that God’s existence, but think for yourself how you come to know something. You can take anything for this example. I will use my own thoughts on this matte to ask how do I know that my thoughts are my own? That question right there asks if that very thought is my own? Did I make up that thought? Did I ask myself, “how do I know that my thoughts are my own? Or did someone else put that thought into my head and did I just think it because it heard it somewhere. It’s possible that somewhere in memory this thought is coming from that. It’s possible that I idid not think that that though on my own that it came from outside of me from someone else. Some else had that thought and maybe they heard it somewhere and passed it on. Who really knows where that thought came from first that question, “how do I know that my thoughts are my own?” It’s a good question that we cannot possibly have an answer for where it came from what matters is that I am asking that question of myself. I’m asking am I thinking this or is someone else or did someone else plant that seed in my mind that now I am asking that question; but again it doesn’t matter because I want to know if I’m actually asking myself that question or not. Am I actually asking myself that question and the answer is no I’m not; I’m really not I don’t really care if my thought is my own. You might think that’s not right you should care if your thoughts are your own. I would say yes I do care that when I express my thoughts I own them; they are mine whether they belong to me or not or originated with me or not they are indeed my thoughts. That is important, but I don’t care about about asking myself if that question, “how do I know that my thoughts are my own?” Is a question that I asked myself or not because it doesn’t matter because asking myself a question about the question I am asking could go on to infinitum because I could ask myself that about everything. I could question my questioning of God’s existence; as if to say the question itself need not exist or maybe that it was put there by someone else. If I make any presupposition about it I am not truly asking the question if God exists because if I come to any premise before asking that question then I am not actually open to the question itself. \n\nIn reality, meaning in this moment of time, not only mine, but yours when you hear these words on this video we are late in the conversation of the existence of God. We are thousands of years late if we are starting from the principle of no preconceived notion. In other words it is impossible to actually come to this question without having some position for some reason and you may not even know what that is. Most likely we don’t know why we think we know what we know; that’s just a not a question that we ask ourselves that often. In the case of God’s existence you could never read everything ever written about God. If God’s existence is real and God were to communicate everything about God and the universe and everything in it and the eyes of man could read it then theoretically man could know everything about all things. I think this is the concern that man has in general that there is a desire to know everything not that it is possible, but to have general common knowledge of everything. This would be the whole of the human enterprise in all of human history that has built the societies that we live in today. If you were to take the whole of all human knowledge and information that could be downloaded into your brain then theoretically you would know everything about everything humans have come to know about everything and you would share that with everyone, so everyone could know everything. It would be the grand opus; the human masterpiece for a period of time, but even that knowledge would fade into the things of this world and might even be lost for all sorts of reasons. Humanity could know everything and lose everything because to know everything and to lose it would be to lose everything. This I think is the tree of knowledge of Good and Evil as it were in the Shared Scriptures. I think there was a moment in time when humanity knew everything all at once and ate from that tree of knowledge and they died a spiritual death then a physical one. I think this is the way it is for some of the most knowledgable, brilliant, and wonderful minds of the ages that in coming to know so much about human thought, knowledge, information and applying it with success can fall into thinking not that they know everything, but that there is only so much they can come know in this life and one might see in his or her legacy the handing on of what they came to discover. \nFor most the most famous that which they handed might be summarized in a sentence or an equation like E=mc2, but not even the educated layman can fully appreciate its meaning. In fact to most people it means nothing and will almost no impact (the knowledge of this equation) will have no impact on their lives or how they live their lives. I think that most people are fakers or have convinced themselves that they believe what they think they believe or proclaim to believe. The means of ones survival often depends on what they claim to believe because a lot of beliefs die out just like their constituents. Time marches on for all of us and no one is spared from death or the misery of life should one not go with the flow of information. Some will argue that we have been on and have gone on the wrong trajectory or that the winners of history are its authors or history is always written by its winners. When in reality the fact is we are where we are in time and space; and this is how it came to be and it probably would not have turned out any differently because the forces of good and evil are always at play and one cannot help, but fight against the other. The distinction might be that evil portrays itself as good and it is evil that suppresses the good while highlighting the evil in the good. We have a mixed bag and \n\n The question is what would you do with all of that knowledge if it was in there; you would use it for your own gain and for that of the world. Everyone knows that the most knowledgable people in the world are aware of what they do not know. There would still be in this who knows everything there is to know at this point in time; there would be the knowledge of what they did not know. For argument’s sake one will not call this God; because not to know something and insert God as a means of explaining what we do not know is not sufficient for what we do not know.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [7985, 264, 5316, 700, 315, 419, 369, 13370, 323, 10650, 2953, 16639, 220, 16, 23, 12, 19, 24, 25, 8224, 21071, 374, 3881, 438, 825, 315, 279, 1429, 20469, 22698, 315, 13587, 11, 323, 374, 264, 6388, 7071, 304, 279, 1532, 84837, 380, 7203, 13, 21071, 374, 7945, 16087, 311, 1128, 566, 19257, 311, 438, 5562, 37244, 16396, 11, 323, 2727, 429, 330, 47, 2122, 2459, 311, 1414, 2513, 825, 3171, 944, 1414, 374, 264, 67276, 315, 8038, 1365, 323, 3602, 432, 374, 279, 2272, 50005, 315, 13587, 2217, 4340, 646, 498, 1414, 2494, 323, 1128, 5421, 1558, 429, 614, 389, 697, 19119, 2272, 1939, 5338, 323, 42680, 911, 12344, 8046, 13, 1260, 15482, 715, 13319, 2989, 12344, 8046, 1052, 374, 198, 58340, 2838, 745, 2155, 3093, 315, 15494, 582, 3887, 304, 624, 3862, 2987, 374, 476, 1052, 4436, 1405, 624, 1072, 8509, 374, 2494, 429, 4041, 3309, 304, 279, 15494, 438, 264, 7286, 3309, 304, 279, 15494, 13, 10230, 52957, 41667, 4650, 438, 303, 1917, 315, 29064, 1882, 389, 1909, 315, 429, 13, 1333, 34151, 46191, 4025, 315, 279, 5025, 1948, 24875, 323, 5382, 7586, 13, 4710, 3862, 374, 264, 8913, 429, 6696, 10000, 311, 9688, 13, 715, 1092, 13320, 374, 12111, 292, 198, 1519, 15482, 3381, 374, 12111, 292, 198, 38, 20808, 748, 57817, 374, 2494, 374, 11259, 4889, 13, 4710, 1109, 23121, 2090, 374, 