INFO 03-28 14:02:48 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-15f4b8d19f744434abd8f1d1abb83b99-0: prompt: 'Business RFP\nFinancial Rationale\nThe financials of the business RFP’s that we received were subdivided and calculated in\nthree parts, first by sleeping room requirements, second by meeting room requirements, and third\nby food and beverage needs. The initial needs of the group were calculated and then services fees\nand taxes were included to give a rough estimation on the Grand Total of the event.\nFigure 1 represents the corporate sleeping room needs. The peak number of room nights\nand total room nights were taken directly from the provided RFP’s, if attrition was not disclosed\nin the RFP we assumed 90% as the standard, however, attrition is a negotiable point if we\ndecided to move forward with the RFP.\nThe Standard Rate was based on the standard rate offered by the hotel for those dates\nfound on Hyatt’s website, and the sleeping room subtotal was determined by multiplying the\nstandard rate by the total room nights. For the group ACC the standard rate is for accomodations\nin Hyatt House, since the group is made up of board members and sponsors we assumed the best\naccommodations would be in the king bedrooms in the Hyatt House and a standard upgrade\nwould be a studio room. For the client we refer to as “Hi Kim” we chose Hyatt Place due to the\nvolume of room nights, and the need for both double and single occupancy rooms which Hyatt\nHouse does not offer. Global Cynergies’ client budgeted $150 per room so we placed them in\nHyatt Place and are able to stay below budget once the group rate discount is applied.\nThe room block discount is set at a standard 15% for all groups. However, this is\nsomething that would be up for negotiation later on. The Group Rate Subtotal is the sleeping\nroom total after the discount has been applied and Total is the final price for sleeping room\naccomodations after sales and accommodations tax are applied (both rates are set by the SC\nDepartment of Revenue). Once the total has been summed up we divided it by the total number\nof room nights to receive the average per person.\nFigure 1\nFigure 2 demonstrates the breakdown for meeting space cost. The meeting space chosen\nis based on the amount of attendees of the event. More detail on meeting and food and beverage\nspecs can be found in the BRM’s in the appendix.\nThe meeting room rates were pulled from the rates on the Sterling Hall cost sheet for\nHyatt House/Place, and then this rate is multiplied by the number of days that the room will be\nreserved and totaled with the combined meeting space required. For example, ACC will need the\nentire Magnolia Ballroom for an 85 person annual meeting. The whole ballroom will be used but\ndivided into two sections. Two of the three sections will be used for the meeting space in banquet\nstyle, and the other third will be divided by a wall and hold the breakfast and lunch buffets that\nguest can then take back to their seats. This gives the client the separation between meeting\nspace and food spreads they requested, and it allows the banquet staff to transition from one meal\nto the next, set-up and clean without disrupting the meeting. This same formula was used for “Hi\nKim” and Global Cynergies, elaborating on what room style would best suit the meeting function\nand finding the appropriately sized space, if there was a time where we were just over the max\ncapacity we always opted for a larger room.\nOnce the subtotal for the room space rental was determined additional service fees were\napplied. The exhibit charge did not apply to any of our RFP’s so it always rang up to zero. The\nhotel service fee comes from the Sterling Hall packet from Hyatt which states that 22% is the\nHotel administrative fee and that all prices and services are subject to this charge. The room\nrental and audio/visual tax rate is 9% as stated in the Sterling Hall packet from Hyatt, and wifi is\noffered complimentary to guests in both their sleeping and meeting rooms. The summation of\nthese taxes and fees with the subtotal of the room rental gives the cost estimation of the meeting\nspace.\nFigure 2\nHyatt can make a profit in addition to sleeping and meeting rooms on food and beverage\noffered during meetings. By adding food and alcohol services Hyatt can require a servers per an\namount of people. For example, the price for a bartender is $125.00 for the first two hours and\neach hour after is an additional $30.00. A bartender is required for every 70 people. Therefore,\nan event that requires a cocktail hour reception can be huge profit for Hyatt.\nA major factor we considered when selecting the RFPs was the ratio between guests and\nsleeping rooms. The amount of sleeping rooms each client needed to the meeting space they take\nup is in correlation to price and profit. For example, " Hi Kim" brings in 136 total room nights, 10\ntotal uses of meeting space and $20,530.60 in food. Food and beverage is another profit making\nservice that Hyatt can provided guests.\nFigure 3\nProduct- Market Match\nACC\nThe Association of Corporate Counsel Chapter Annual Meeting is an event that is for a\ngroup based out of Columbia, SC and hosts about 75-100 guests from throughout the state.\nPrevious hosts have both been in Columbia, South Carolina.\nSince the group is already from South Carolina the\nseasonality is not something that will sway their choice to\ncome here, the weather for Charleston in September is\nsomething they are accustomed to and the rate for this time\nof year is comparable to rates you would receive in\nColumbia. Because this corporation and the attendees are\nfrom the state it will be easy to sell them on the destination\nof Charleston because, it is a departure from where the event\nwas the last two years and the meeting will still be in-state but will provide new opportunities.\nThe amount of rooms and meeting space they are requesting is reasonable, especially\nbecause it is taking place in a month that is hard to sell. ACC would take up 21% of Hyatt Place\nsleeping rooms, and occupy 25% of the meeting rooms over the course of the stay cumulatively\n(shown in Figure A ). This presents about a 1:1 ratio for sleeping rooms to meeting rooms. This\ngroup is coming on shoulder nights (Sunday-Tuesday) and during the hot summer months, when\nsales are low. This is a good ratio because it leaves plenty of empty space in the combined Hyatt\nHouse/Place to sell to additional corporate groups, and enough meeting space as well. The group\nwill also require catering and A/V needs, the A/V needs will provide a high margin along with\nthe sleeping rooms that is attractive.\nBecause ACC wanted to book their visit during the low season we extend the offer of the\ngroup rate three days post which carry into the weekend. We assumed most will decide not to\nstay, but those that do will give us valuable room nights for September weekdays.\nThe group is one we highly considered because the RFP language is clear and concise, it\nis easy to read and the group appears like they will be easy to plan with since they have a vision\nfor what they want for layout and itinerary.\n"Hi Kim"\nThe most important note to make on the group we refer to as “Hi Kim” is they are second-hold\nfor these same dates. They are great business opportunity but\nthere is another group currently in negotiations for the same\nweekend that- we assume- requires similar needs as this group\nwhich would not allow both groups to book at Hyatt.\nThe group “Hi Kim” is coming during the high season of\nCharleston, near the end of May, and does not include their\nmeeting history or information about their group. Based on the\nfamiliarity in the RFP letter we assume this group is familiar\nwith Charleston and the prices they generally requested of\ngroups, especially in May, and the group is aware they are on\nhold but is still eager to come visit so they would be a group\neasy to sell to and negotiate with.\nThe group requires 136 room nights in Hyatt Place, 24% of the hotel’s sleeping rooms,\nand 31% of the cumulative meeting rooms (shown in Figure B ). The rooms ratio is close to 1:1\nfor sleeping rooms to meeting rooms. The group would be occupy a large portion of the hotel’s\nsleeping roms cumulatively. This is a large amount of sleeping room to occupy for the high\nseason with a low amount of sleeping rooms. Given that the time of the stay is during\nCharleston’s high season, a group with a 1:1 ratio is very practical, especially given high amount\nof leisure travelers.\nThe group “Hi Kim” would need substantial A/V, and food & beverage needs, the A/V\nwould provide high margins however, food & beverage is not one of the better profit drivers for\nthe hotel. Since another piece of business is already on the books for this time of year we did not\noffer them a proposal. However, this is a group that we would like to build a relationship with\nand suggest booking with us at a later date.\nGlobal Cynergies’ Client\nGlobal Cynergies is a third party representing an unnamed client. The meeting is for a small\ngroup of young corporate marketing leaders looking for a destination with fun restaurants and activities\nnearby. This is the first time the meeting is being held. The group is considering Nashville, Asheville, and\nAdairsville as other options, all comparable to Charleston in one way or another but lacking the whole\npackage that only Charleston can offer. The group would be a\ngood match for the Charleston Hyatt because the area is\nsurrounding is youth-centric, there’s an endless supply of bars\nand restaurants for anyone’s taste, all a walkable distance\nfrom the hotel, including the Pour Taproom on top of the\nH yatt. Even though this group is comprised of only 12\npeople, it is a young group and they are a marketing\nleadership team, hinting that there is potential for the\nteam/company will grow and become a repeat client.\nThe group wants to come on shoulder nights in\nAugust, which are generally hard to fill nights in a low\nseason for Charleston, these dates would be a nice piece\nof business to bring in and fill late summer holes. The group has 24 total room nights in the\nHyatt Place, taking up a total of 6% of the hotel’s sleeping rooms but they require 3 full days of\nmeeting space in the Carolina room equaling about 8% of meeting rooms, (shown in Figure C ).\nThis is about a 1:1 ratio of sleeping rooms to meeting rooms, but still a large percentage of\nmeeting rooms and sleeping rooms would remain open. The hotel will have plenty of sleeping\nrooms to provide to leisure travelers and the meeting space they are occupying will be fairly\ninconsequential if other groups require meeting space at the same time.\nThe group will also provide a high profit margin because they need rooms and A/V\nrequirements, and they will also require food & beverages throughout the whole day of their\nmeetings charged per consumption.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [22727, 431, 11698, 198, 56230, 431, 37035, 198, 785, 5896, 82, 315, 279, 2562, 431, 11698, 748, 429, 582, 3949, 1033, 66509, 4490, 323, 16588, 304, 198, 27856, 5479, 11, 1156, 553, 21127, 3054, 8502, 11, 2086, 553, 6438, 3054, 8502, 11, 323, 4843, 198, 1694, 3607, 323, 42350, 3880, 13, 576, 2856, 3880, 315, 279, 1874, 1033, 16588, 323, 1221, 3516, 12436, 198, 437, 13125, 1033, 5230, 311, 2968, 264, 11165, 41204, 389, 279, 10304, 10657, 315, 279, 1538, 624, 22029, 220, 16, 10868, 279, 12870, 21127, 3054, 3880, 13, 