INFO 03-28 14:02:53 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-74b9551a53154a4a949d60ddff3e16eb-0: prompt: 'Imagine you are a sales coach, provide a detailed summary of the call for the sales manager. Use a sales framework and make sure to recap next steps.\n\nTime,Summary\n0:00 - 3:02,"Deenagh Robare talks about how their camera sometimes stalls when they try to turn it on.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they would love to understand their story at Arrive.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they used to use Lattice for one on ones, but there are a lot of limitations around that.\nDeenagh Robare talks about how people started the word about a free subscription, so they were getting above their allotted users.\nDeenagh Robare says they ended up going with Peacon, which is a Lattice competitor."\n3:04 - 4:40,"Deenagh Robare says a bunch of them had been using Fellow for over six months, and they love it.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that Lattice is a cool tool, but it\'s hard to get everyone to use it.\nMarcus de Verteuil says it\'s easier to get people to default to it for their one on ones meetings.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they can track and add notes to any meeting."\n4:40 - 6:33,"Marcus de Verteuil asks Deenagh Robare about their journey within the HR team, and Deenagh Robare says they\'re an HR apps manager at Arrive.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they spent the last ten years in the logistics industry, and they ran operations and logistics for a wine company, then moved back to Chicago and started working for Arrive.\nDeenagh Robare says they spent the majority of their time overseeing the operations team, then moved into project management."\n6:33 - 8:57,"Deenagh Robare notes that they spent a year doing that as a business partner, and this new HR operations role came up.\nDeenagh Robare says they moved into this role in January, and started deploying Pecon and Fellow.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they hired an Hrs systems manager and integration manager, and they haven\'t talked with them about Fellow yet because it\'s been going well.\nDeenagh Robare says they should keep an eye on their active users, and they could partner with HRBPs to get more utilization from managers."\n8:57 - 10:53,"Deenagh Robare says they\'re implementing a new compliance training system, cybersecurity harassment training, and code of conduct training.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they did a full revamp of their handbook, and they\'re looking at their onboarding and offboarding experience.\nDeenagh Robare talks about how their operations and project management background helps push these things over the finish line."\n10:53 - 13:15,"Deenagh Robare notes that they are on a growth path, but they\'re also looking at their efficiencies.\nDeenagh Robare says they develop their own technology in house, but they\'re also using a lot of outside platforms.\nDeenagh Robare mentions that Fellow integrates with Jira, and they don\'t think all the right people were in the room when they made the decision to go with Lattice.\nMarcus de Verteuil talks about how the adoption with Fellow has been quite organic, and they\'ve done a good job at allowing people to use and interpret it."\n13:16 - 15:23,"Deenagh Robare notes that they connected it to Google Calendar.\nDeenagh Robare says they haven\'t used it yet to encourage one on ones.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that it\'s just a matter of getting comfortable with it and using it for whatever meetings you can.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they shared the agenda with them through their Fellow Cross Workspace collaboration.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that they have a graph of usage and stats they can look at together, which allows them to better collaborate with folks who are at different companies that also use Fellow."\n15:23 - 17:09,"Marcus de Verteuil notes that they pulled from the last month the number of users who are using the tool.\nMarcus de Verteuil asks if they had a plan as to which departments they would go after, and Deenagh Robare says their user group was Leadership and Managers.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they want to keep an eye on who\'s a continuous active user versus who used it for a week or isn\'t logging in anymore."\n17:10 - 19:12,"Deenagh Robare says they anticipated being in the middle tier of up to 1000 seats, and that\'s the cost they have set aside for this year.\nDeenagh Robare asks how they can keep an eye on how many active users they actually have on a recurring basis, and Marcus de Verteuil notes that they base it off of the monthly recurring active users.\nMarcus de Verteuil mentions that the usage stats can be found in the dashboard, but they don\'t think they\'ll hit over 1000 this year."\n19:12 - 21:00,"Marcus de Verteuil notes that they\'ll make sure they can limit it to the most important 1000 users in this year.\nDeenagh Robare mentions that if they could set it up where they\'re not going into the third tier this year, they can talk about it next year.\nDeenagh Robare says they\'ve only had it set up for a month, but monthly active.\nMarcus de Verteuil mentions that 715 is showing those who just popped in and have not kept using it, and that shows there\'s 35 people who popped into Fellow."\n21:00 - 23:13,"Marcus de Verteuil notes that they almost have 500 people on a weekly basis, which is awesome, but they can\'t financially go past the tier level.\nMarcus de Verteuil asks if they know which cohorts are new people that have joined, and if they want to get to 1000, what are the best departments to go after and provide consistent training to.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they don\'t know who these people are, and asks if there is a way to get an overview.\nMarcus de Verteuil says that would be something they could potentially sync if that\'s set up in workday."\n23:14 - 25:04,"Marcus de Verteuil asks if Okta has manager, report, and people\'s titles, and Marcus de Verteuil notes that they can try to pull a report.\nMarcus de Verteuil says if 80% or 70% of the sales team is using it, they can get the rest of the team on board and make it a standard thing.\nDeenagh Robare asks how to put a roadblock in place if they go up in number of users."\n25:05 - 26:29,"Marcus de Verteuil says they could change the way people can add other teammates to the workspace.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that they can create limitations in terms of who can invite who, but they don\'t know if that would make it difficult for managers to run their meetings.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they can respect the 1000 tier if they\'re saying they can\'t go past it."\n26:30 - 28:17,"Marcus de Verteuil notes that they have an invoice for the first tier of users, and it\'s for the year.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they\'d pay for the remaining months in that year, and phase three could kick in next year.\nMarcus de Verteuil mentions that gives them time to plan and push adoption.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they can focus on building a policy on how people use Fellow.\nDeenagh Robare notes that they can talk to their leadership if they\'re starting to get closer to the 1000."\n28:17 - 29:46,"Marcus de Verteuil says they can respect Deenagh Robare\'s planning of deploying the tool.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that they will do a deep dive into your users and try to identify insights, and they could set up trainings with leaders in those groups for specific use cases.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they have a couple of templates they can build off of.\nDeenagh Robare notes that their tech teams use Confluence, but they have an intranet, called the Arrive Feed."\n29:47 - 32:04,"Deenagh Robare mentions that it\'s in WordPress, and they\'re working on building a manager specific page in their intranet.\nMarcus de Verteuil says they\'ll get insights into users, and they\'ll get a template for how to build the Internet page.\nMarcus de Verteuil notes that they\'ve surpassed the initial band of users, and they want to make sure they\'re aware of that.\nMarcus de Verteuil mentions that to fo