INFO 03-28 14:02:54 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-ddcb9adb45d74d81a056df9c2aa33bb5-0: prompt: 'You are an AI programming assistant. Read very carefully the following problem and do everything that the problem asks you too, you must do ALL the assignments in this problem. \nFirst think step-by-step - analyze the problem and lay out a detailed summary of everything that you are asked to do (keep in mind that you need not only to write code but also do everything else the problem asks you to) in great detail and don’t forget to include constraints and requirements for the given problem .\nThen execute your plan of what you need to do and output the code in a single code block, writing out the full implementation of every function and file. Minimize any other prose. It will be too long for a single message, so split it into several messages. I will ask you to “go on” and you will need to continue writing your answer from where you left off.\nFormulation of the problem:\nThere is a multiprocessor computing system (CS) with identical processor nodes connected by a network. A set of programs that must be executed on this computing system is specified. Each program runs on one processor; it is permissible to execute several programs on the same processor. Some programs exchange data at a fixed rate. Each program creates a fixed load on the processor. For each processor, the upper limit of the permissible load on it is set.\nPrograms running on different processors communicate over the network; this creates a load on the network, measured in KB/s. Programs running on the same processor communicate through the processor\'s local memory; such an exchange does not create a load on the network.\nTo minimize network latency and improve system scalability, programs are distributed across processors so that network load is minimal.\nYou are given:\n1. the number of processors in the computing system;\n2. for each processor - the upper limit of the load (in %, no more than 100);\n3. the number of programs;\n4. for each program: processor load (in %, no more than 100);\n5. a list of pairs of programs between which data is exchanged;\n6. for each such pair of programs: data exchange rate (KB/s).\nYou are required: \nto distribute all programs among processors in such a way as to minimize the load on the network, and at the same time not violate the restrictions on the load on the processors.\nThe solution to the problem is represented as a vector 𝑥̅ (its size is equal to the number of programs), in which for each program the number of the processor to which it is distributed to is indicated.\nTo simplify the algorithms for solving the problem and generating sets of input data for the study of algorithms, the following additional restrictions are imposed on the input data:\n1. the upper limit of the processor load can take values of 50, 70, 90, 100 (%);\n2. the load on the processor created by the program can take values of 5, 10, 15, 20 (%);\n3. the intensity of data exchange between programs can be equal to 0 (no exchange), 10, 50, 70, 100 (KB/s). \n\nYou need to use “Random search algorithm with region narrowing for program distribution among processors”.\nThis algorithm iteratively randomly generates a solution to the problem 𝑥̅ and checks whether it is better in terms of the value of the objective function 𝐹(𝑥̅) (i.e. network load) than the best of the previously found correct (satisfying the constraints) solutions. If better, then the correctness of the generated solution is checked; if correct, this solution becomes the new best.\nThe execution scheme of the basic algorithm is as follows:\n1. calculate the theoretical maximum load on the network (for the case when all data exchange goes through the network);\n2. set the current best value of the objective function (we denote it by 𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡) to the value calculated at step 1;\n3. randomly generate a solution to the problem (vector 𝑥̅);\n4. if 𝐹(𝑥̅) ≥ 𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡, go to step 3 (the generated solution is no better than the previously found one)\n5. if 𝑥̅ is an incorrect solution (violates processor load limits), go to step 3;\n// at step 6 we have a correct solution, better than the previously found one\n6. update the current best solution and the current best value of the objective function:\n𝑥̅𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡 ≔ 𝑥̅; 𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡 ≔ 𝐹(𝑥̅);\n7. go to step 3.\nThe algorithm terminates when a correct solution with zero value is found\n𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡, or when the value of 𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡 was not improved after 5000 attempts in a row, i.e. the program was unable to find a correct solution 𝑥̅ is such that 𝐹(𝑥̅) < 𝐹𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡. If during the execution of the algorithm it was not possible to find any correct solution, the result of the algorithm should be an array containing only values (-1).\nAlso implement a Parallel algorithm: the algorithm works in the form of several parallel\n"threads", each of which performs the same actions as the underlying algorithm. Threads update a single (common for them) best solution and the best value of the objective function.\n\nInput data format:\nThe set of input data is specified in the file. The file format must be based on XML. The specific file structure (XML elements, their attributes) needs to be developed by you and be written out.\n\nOutput format:\nYou need to output to standard output.\nIn case of success:\n1. string "success";\n2. number of algorithm iterations;\n3. elements of the found solution 𝑥̅𝑏𝑒𝑠𝑡, with a space separator;\n4. network load value for the found solution.\nThe text for each of paragraphs 1, 2, 3 should be displayed on a separate line. \nIn case of failure:\n1. the string "failure";\n2. the number of iterations of the algorithm.\n\nAssignments:\nMain task (mandatory, 5 points):\n1. implement the basic algorithm for solving the problem in the form of a program in Python;\n2. check its work on datasets with 4, 8, 16 processors; average programs per processor (number\\_of\\_programs / number\\_of\\_processors) at least 8; average processor load of at least 50%; each program communicates with at least two other programs; in each set of input data there must be all variants of the upper limit of the processor load, data exchange intensity indicated in the Problem Statement; (data sets must be listed along with the program) \n3. write instructions for the program:\nhow to build and run the program;\ndescription of the input file format.\nRequirements for software implementation:\n1. the program must be implemented in Python;\n2. instructions must be in .txt format;\n3. the text of the program should be well readable, structured, and contain comments in the amount necessary for its understanding;\n4. the program should diagnose and handle situations of incorrect user input and the contents of input files;', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [2610, 525, 458, 15235, 15473, 17847, 13, 4457, 1602, 15516, 279, 2701, 3491, 323, 653, 4297, 429, 279, 3491, 17064, 498, 2238, 11, 498, 1969, 653, 13097, 279, 31172, 304, 419, 3491, 13, 715, 5338, 1744, 3019, 14319, 29208, 481, 23643, 279, 3491, 323, 10962, 700, 264, 11682, 12126, 315, 4297, 429, 498, 525, 4588, 311, 653, 320, 13096, 304, 3971, 429, 498, 1184, 537, 1172, 311, 3270, 2038, 714, 1083, 653, 4297, 770, 279, 3491, 17064, 498, 311, 8, 304, 2244, 7716, 323, 1513, 1405, 10667, 311, 2924, 16982, 323, 8502, 369, 279, 2661, 3491, 16448, 12209, 9026, 697, 3119, 315, 1128, 498, 1184, 311, 653, 323, 2550, 279, 2038, 304, 264, 3175, 2038, 2504, 11, 4378, 700, 279, 2480, 8129, 315, 1449, 729, 323, 1034, 13, 3386, 11853, 894, 1008, 60701, 13, 1084, 