INFO 03-28 14:03:11 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-df12bf105d5f47568ac6c8285e737256-0: prompt: "AGE-RELATED\nEYE DISEASES\nMD UNDERGRADUATE EDUCATION\nWEEK 67: DEMENTIA\nDR. GRACE QIAO MD FRCSC\nPROVIDENCE HEALTH CARE\nDEPARTMENT OF OPHTHALMOLOGY \nDISCLOSURES\nI have no disclosures.\n(These slides are adapted from Dr Stephanie’s Wise delivery\nof this lecture from previous years)\nACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nI respectfully acknowledge that I live and work on the\nunceded homelands of the xʷmәθkʷәy̓әm (Musqueam),\nSkwxwú7mesh Úxwumixw (Squamish), and sәl̓ilwәtaɁɬ (Tsleil\nWaututh) First Nations. \nOBJECTIVES\n1. Review ocular anatomy\n2. Describe the natural aging changes in the eye including vitreal\nchanges, refractive error, presbyopia and cataract\n3. List and describe the risk factors, signs and symptoms, physician\nexam findings, diagnosis and management of the following age-related\neye diseases:\n• Cataract\n• Posterior vitreal detachment\n• Glaucoma\n• Age related macular degeneration (ARMD)\n4. Recognize the current provincial visual regulations for obtaining and\nmaintaining drivers licensure in BC\n5. List available resources and tools for low vision patients\n6. Considerations for the ophthalmic exam in geriatric populations \nANATOMY \nANATOMY \nNORMAL FUNDUS\nABNORMAL FUNDUS\nTsui, Jade\n1998-03-04 (18), Clinic/Doctor : IRIS Optometry Kitsilano 674\nOS Pupil size 4.1mm Field: CENTRAL NASAL Date: 2016-07-20 10:45 AM\nNotes:\nTsui, Jade\n1998-03-04 (18), Clinic/Doctor : IRIS Optometry Kitsilano 674\nOD Pupil size 4.8mm Field: CENTRAL NASAL Date: 2016-07-20 10:45 AM\nNotes:\nTHE ENTIRE FUNDUS\nREFRACTIVE ERROR\nMain focusing powers of the eye\n1. Cornea\n• Main focusing power\n• Adds 35-45 D of converging power\n2. Lens\n• Adds 20 D of converging power\n• Allows for accommodation (near vision) by changing\nshape \nREFRACTION TERMINOLOGY\nEmmetropia – “normal”\nMyopia – nearsightedness\nHyperopia – farsightedness\nAstigmatism – refractive powers in different axes\n(think of a football) \nMYOPIA VS HYPEROPIA\nUncorrected myopia Uncorrected hyperopia\nCorrected myopia (minus lens) Corrected hyperopia (plus lens) \nMyopia: minus\nlenses, minify eyes\nHyperopia: plus lenses,\nmagnify eyes\nPRESBYOPIA\nPresbyopia: with aging\nthere is loss of the ability of\nthe lens to change shape\n(loss of accommodation)\nAccommodation: ability of\nthe ciliary muscle to\ncontract and the lens to\nbecome more convex \nPRESBYOPIA\n• Clinically important in early to mid 40s and\nonwards\n• Symptoms\n• Decreased ability to see near objects\n• Distance vision is unchanged\n• Treatment: plus lenses \nCATARACT\n• Any opacity or discoloration of the\nlens – usually reserved for opacities\nthat affect visual acuity\n• Cataracts are described based on\nareas in the lens that are affected\n• Sub-capsular\n• Cortical\n• Nuclear\n• Can be secondary to:\n• Aging – most common\n• Congenital or genetic anomaly\n• Systemic diseases\n• Trauma, radiation, medications\n• Age related cataract\n• 50% of people 50-75 yrs old\n• 70% of people over 75 yrs old \nCATARACT\n• Symptoms:\n• Gradual onset of decreased vision\n• Difficulties in dim or bright light\n• Halos and glare\n• Monocular diplopia\n• Signs\n• Decreased visual acuity\n• Increase in myopia –”second sight”\n• Really means ‘better near vision’\n• Due to increase in refractive power of denser lens nucleus\n• No pupillary signs (no relative afferent pupillary defect )\n• Opacification or discoloration of lens on direct\nophthalmoscopy \nCATARACT\nCATARACT\nDiagnosis:\n• Full eye examination\n• Assess for alternate causes\nof vision loss\nManagement:\n• Observation – correct\nrefractive error and follow\n• Surgery – cataract\nextraction and intraocular\nlens implantation\n• When symptomatic and\nthe visual acuity is\n<20/50 or resulting in an\nimpairment in function \nVITREOUS AND AGING\n• What is the vitreous?\n• Transparent connective tissue,\noccupies the space in the\nposterior chamber\n• Connections to posterior lens,\noptic nerve and retina\n• With aging there is liquefaction\nand collapse of the vitreous\n• When this occurs, it can\ndetach from posterior\nstructures\n• “Posterior vitreal detachment” \nPOSTERIOR VITREAL\nDETACHMENT\n• If it happens gradually and uniformly → symptoms are mild\n• If the forces of separation are strong → can lead to retinal tear (in 8-\n10%) → untreated, can progress to a retinal detachment\n• Symptoms:\n• Floaters, flashes\n• If there is distorted or decreased vision, there maybe a vitreous\nhemorrhage, retinal detachment, or other pathology\n• Signs:\n• Vision should be preserved, no pupillary findings\n• Near translucent vitreal opacity above optic nerve (slit lamp)\n• Risk factors\n• Increased age – after 60 yrs old\n• Myopia (increased axial length of eye)\n• Trauma\n• Previous cataract surgery\n• Inflammatory conditions (e.g. vitritis) \nPOSTERIOR VITREAL\nDETACHMENT\n• Diagnosis\n• Dilated fundus\nexamination to rule out\nretinal tear/detachment\n• Management\n• Counselling\n• Non sight threatening (if no retinal tear)\n• Symptoms subside in most patients within a few months\n• Most patients develop same in fellow eye within 1 year\n• Return to clinic for repeat examination if there is a\nincrease in floaters, flashing lights, decrease in vision or\nvisual field defect (concern for retinal tear or detachment)\n• Follow-up\n• Repeat dilated fundus exam within 4-6 weeks or urgently if\nthere is a change in symptoms \nGLAUCOMA\n• The term glaucoma refers to a group of heterogenous\ndisorders\n• All glaucomas have the hallmark of progressive optic\nneuropathy and loss of visual field\n• This loss of vision is irreversible but often preventable\n• Defining feature of glaucoma = glaucomatous optic\nneuropathy\n• Characterized by damage to the optic nerve (cupping)\n• Visual field defects resulting from progressive optic nerve\ndamage\n• Initially often peripheral (asymptomatic)\n• Central vision is often preserved until late in disease\ncourse (too late… screening is crucial!)\nGLAUCOMA\nIs classified based on the morphology of the drainage angle\n• Open\n• Closed\n• What is open or closed?\nGLAUCOMA\nOPEN ANGLE GLAUCOMA\n• Insidious onset\n• Risk factors: ↑IOP, ↑age, African or\nHispanic descent, family history,\nthinner corneal thickness, myopia,\ntrauma, steroid use\n• Symptoms: painless, slow\nprogressive peripheral vision loss\n• Signs:\n• +/- Relative afferent pupillary\ndefect\n• +/- Elevated intraocular\npressure (IOP)\n• Constricted visual fields\n• Optic nerve appearance\n(cupping, hemorrhage,\nnotching)\nCLOSED ANGLE GLAUCOMA\n• Sudden onset\n• Risk factors: Inuit or East Asian\ndescent, hyperopia, ↑age, female sex,\nfamily history\n• Symptoms: pain, nausea, decreased\nvision, halos\n• Signs:\n• Very elevated IOP\n• Fixed mid-dilated pupil\n• Significantly reduced visual acuity\n• Red and injected conjunctiva\n• Tearing\n• Shallow anterior chamber\n• Hazy cornea\nGLAUCOMA\nGLAUCOMA\nWhat is the CUP? \nGLAUCOMA \nGLAUCOMA\n• A word on intraocular pressure (IOP)\n• Elevated IOP is one of the most important risk factors for\nopen angle glaucoma\n• Elevated IOP alone is not sufficient to make a diagnosis\n• Diagnosis\n• Open angle glaucoma – depends on the optic nerve and\nvisual field appearance\n• Closed angle glaucoma – depends on examination\nfindings of a closed angle, increased IOP and resulting\noptic neuropathy \nGLAUCOMA\n• Serial visual field tests\nreveal progressive loss\n(left eye - blind spot is\ntemporal)\nGLAUCOMA\nManagement: despite the many risk factors\nin glaucoma, the only modifiable risk factor is\nIOP\nMedical Therapy\n• Topical IOP lowering agents\n• Example: latanoprost\n• Prostaglandin analogue\n• MOA: works by increasing spaces\nbetween muscle fascicles in ciliary\nbody, thereby increasing uveoscleral\noutflow\nLaser Therapy\n• Laser trabeculoplasty\n• Laser peripheral iridotomy\nSurgical Therapy\n• Fistulizing procedures\n• Microinvasive procedures (stents)\nARMD\n• Age-related macular degeneration\n• Incurable, bilateral, acquired\ndegenerative disease of the macula\n• Leading cause of irreversible central\nvisual loss (<20/200) among people\n>age 50\n• Leads to central vision disturbances\n• Can lead to inability to drive, difficulty\nrecognizing faces and significant\nchallenges with ADLs\n• 2 forms: dry and wet\nARMD – DRY\nMost common form – 90% of cases\n• Slowly progressive\n• Less significant visual impairment\n• Characterized by drusen (granular lipid rich material) and\natrophic pigmentary changes\nARMD – WET\nPresence of choroidal neovascular\nmembrane\n• Rapidly progressive\n• Leads to significant central visual loss\n• Characterized by macular hemorrhage and macular\nedema\nARMD\n• Risk factors\n• Age (>55)\n• Female sex\n• Light coloured iris\n• Family history\n• Smoking, hypertension\n• Symptoms\n• Many early dry ARMD patients remain asymptomatic\n• Blurred vision, gradual or sudden\n• Distortion or metamorphopsia\n• Central or paracentral scotoma (“blind spots”)\n• Signs\n• Decreased visual acuity\n• Amsler grid test findings\n• Drusen, pigmentary changes or choroidal neovascular membrane in\nmacula \nARMD\nMetamorphopsia Central scotoma\n\uf0df Amsler Grid\nARMD\n• Diagnosis\n• Macular examination\n• Optical Coherence Tomography scanning\n• IV fluorescein angiogram (leakage of dye from vessels)\nARMD\nManagement\n• Dry:\n• Smoking cessation, improve dietary intake of\ngreen leafy vegetables, manage hypertension, UV\nprotection\n• AREDS2 vitamin supplementation\n• Vit C, Vit E, zinc, copper, lutein, zeaxanthin\n• Decreases rate of progression from intermediate\nARMD to advanced ARMD\n• Low vision aids\n• Regular Amsler grid testing to detect acute vision\nchanges → urgent review with ophthalmologist\n• Wet:\n• Above, plus…\n• Intraocular injections of anti-vascular endothelial\ngrowth factors (anti-VEGF)\n• Vision stabilizes and often improves as leakage\nfrom abnormal vessels is stopped \nDRIVING VISION STANDARDS\nVisual Acuity\n>20/50 with both eyes open & examined together\n• Commercial: 20/40 or better\n• Emergency: 20/30 or better\n• 20/60 or 20/70 - RoadSafetyBC may request an on-road\nassessment\n• <20/70 - usually won't request on-road assessment\n• Restriction: corrective lenses required\nDRIVING VISION STANDARDS\nVisual Field\n• 120’ continuous horizontally, and 15’ continuous above and below\nhorizontal meridian\n• Commercial: same\n• Emergency: 150' horizontal, 20' above & below fixation\nDRIVING VISION STANDARDS\nA Physician’s Role\n• Assess measures of visual function\n• Consider other medical conditions that may be additive to vision\nimpairment:\n• Substance use, dementia, sleep disorders, neurologic disorders or\ndysfunction (seizures, stroke), brain injury, endocrine/ metabolic disorders,\nMSK disability\nReporting\n• Section 230 of the Motor Vehicle Act: report to RoadSafetyBC any patient\nwho has a medical condition that makes it dangerous for them to drive AND\ncontinues to drive after you’ve warned them not to\n• Physician is protected from any legal action brought for making a report\n• Physician can be found liable for failing to report in provinces with mandatory\nrequirements if a patient of theirs causes damage or injury\nLOW VISION\nPatients with vision loss from any ocular disease may require\nvision rehabilitation\n• WHO?\n• Any patient with vision impairment that is not treatable\n(refer after evaluation by ophthalmologist)\n• WHAT?\n• Low Vision Clinic at VGH Eye Care Centre\n• Canadian National Institute for the Blind (CNIB)\n• Introduction and demonstration of lighting, contrast and aid\ndevices\n• Mobility and orientation training \nLOW VISION \nOPHTHALMIC EXAM IN\nGERIATRIC PATIENTS\nBarriers to care\n• Communication\n• Accessibility/transport\n• Cognitive function\n• Diagnosis\n• Treatment\n• Follow up\nOPHTHALMIC EXAM IN\nGERIATRIC PATIENTS\nOffice accessibility\n• Parking, elevators\n• Walking distance\nWaiting room and exam room considerations\n• Mobility limitations\n• Walker/wheelchair accommodations\nEquipment\n• Near visual acuity testing\n• Hand held slit lamp\n• Limited testing due to positional limitations\nPatient support person\nBE FLEXIBLE, DO YOUR BEST\nOPHTHALMIC EXAM IN\nGERIATRIC PATIENTS\nMay be unable to measures VA\n• Rely on observation of activities, functioning, ADLs\nMay need accommodations for treatments/surgery\nWE COVERED A LOT OF GROUND!\n• Anatomy\n• Refractive error\n• Presbyopia\n• Posterior vitreal detachment\n• Cataract\n• Glaucoma\n• Age-related macular degeneration\n• Driving standards\n• Low vision\n• Ophthalmic exam in geriatric\npatients \nANY QUESTIONS?", params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [3585, 12, 34320, 198, 36, 59644, 422, 9133, 4066, 50, 198, 6076, 56218, 8626, 1808, 52, 2336, 92977, 3495, 198, 54, 32262, 220, 21, 22, 25, 422, 16432, 5863, 198, 7687, 13, 14773, 5576, 1207, 5863, 46, 13979, 434, 7380, 3540, 198, 9117, 7483, 10150, 72625, 62327, 198, 1150, 78365, 3008, 13134, 2545, 31714, 10531, 51432, 715, 21202, 34, 37558, 50661, 198, 40, 614, 902, 74892, 624, 7, 9485, 21521, 525, 29364, 504, 2926, 48143, 748, 61001, 9691, 198, 1055, 419, 30578, 504, 3681, 1635, 340, 4032, 96522, 38, 16432, 50, 198, 40, 92345, 24645, 429, 358, 3887, 323, 975, 389, 279, 198, 1347, 29935, 4998, 301, 2844, 315, 279, 856, 149220, 76, 145004, 88538, 74, 149220, 145004, 88, 136, 241, 145004, 76, 320, 61598, 591, 309, 1326, 19290, 20984, 86, 6654, 22, 23506, 1683, 248, 87, 86, 372, 941, 86, 320, 50, 86701, 812, 701, 323, 274, 145004, 75, 