INFO 03-28 14:04:02 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-7f0057ad0363407c8e5ef2e34a83990d-0: prompt: 'Convert the following text passage into 25 dot points, keeping in statutes and cases\n\n2.2.1.3 Unfavourable witnesses\n\nA party usually calls a witness because she anticipates that the witness will testify in a way that helps the party’s case. But this is not always the case, as some parties are ethically obliged to call certain witnesses. And sometimes the witness’s testimony ends up helping the other side, or being not much help to anyone.\n\nThe uniform evidence legislation permits a court to free a party from the usual restraints on examination in chief if the witness the party called meets any of three conditions set out in section 38(1):\n\nA party who called a witness may, with the leave of the court, question the witness, as though the party were cross-examining the witness, about:\n\n(a) evidence given by the witness that is unfavourable to the party; or\n\n(b) a matter of which the witness may reasonably be supposed to have knowledge about and about which it appears to the court the witness is not, in examination-in-chief, making a genuine attempt to give evidence; or\n\n(c) whether the witness has, at any time, made a prior inconsistent statement.\n\nThese three conditions respectively involve what the witness did say, the witness’s apparent motivations and what the witness has said previously. The three wings of section 38(1) do not limit each other. So, a witness’s evidence can be ‘unfavourable’ even if she is genuinely trying to recall the evidence and hasn’t said anything different in the past. The other two paragraphs do not require that the witness’s evidence detract from the party’s case, but simply that the witness either isn’t making a genuine attempt to recall (even if what the witness says is neutral or favourable, and doesn’t contradict her earlier evidence) or said something different in the past (even if the witness is making a genuine attempt to remember and what she says is neutral or helpful.)\n\nThe word ‘unfavourable’ in section 38(1)(a) is not defined. Victoria’s Court of Appeal has observed:\n\nWhether evidence is unfavourable to the case that the party is seeking to prove will depend upon the circumstances of each case. The party’s case may be discerned from its opening, its pleadings and/or the evidence which the court has already heard or which is proposed to be called. If the evidence of the witness called by that party is inconsistent with, or ‘likely to be contradictory’ of, that identified case, it will ordinarily satisfy the description of ‘unfavourable’. The inconsistency or contradictory nature of the evidence may arise because the witness asserts a fact—or fails to give evidence of a fact—in circumstances that permit the conclusion that his evidence is unfavourable. Evidence may be nfavourable even if the witness whom it is sought to cross-examine gives no evidence that actually detracts from the case of the party by whom he or she is \ncalled. If the party calling the witness contends that the witness should be able to give evidence supportive of that party’s case, and the witness does not give such evidence, that may suffice to make the evidence given by the witness ‘unfavourable’ within the meaning of s 38.70\n\nA police officer’s evidence that the accused, a fellow officer, did not have any \nopportunity to assault the victim and that the victim’s injuries were from falling over, was ‘a very clear example of evidence which is ‘unfavourable’ in this sense’.71 In Asling, discussed above, the only part of the witness’s testimony that satisfied this test was his statement that the accused did not tell him where to find the car that was used to commit the murder. His other omissions (about admissions the accused allegedly made) were merely neutral.\n\nBeing permitted to ‘cross-examine’ the witness means that the party can ask the \nwitness questions that suggest an answer, avoiding the rules on leading questions \ndiscussed above. Section 38(1) provides that leave can only be granted to ask ‘about’ whichever of the three matters listed in that section are satisfied. For example, if leave is granted because the witness made an inconsistent statement, then leave is limited to asking about that statement. However, in R v Le, 72 Heydon JA commented that questioning can also be:\n\ndirected to establishing the probability of the factual state of affairs in relation to those subjects contended for by the party conducting the questioning or the improbability of the witness’s evidence on those subjects. In establishing the probability or improbability of one or other state of affairs, the questioner is entitled to ask questions about matters going only to credibility with a view to shaking the witness’s credibility on the s 38(1) subjects.\n\nFor example, questions can be asked to show not only that the earlier statement was made, but also that it was accurate and the witness’s subsequent account was not.\n\nAlthough it is usually only the opposing party that can ask questions that are solely \nabout a witness’s credibility (see Chapter 11: Credibility), the judge can permit such \nquestions in the case of a party’s own witness, but section 38(3) specifies that this \nrequires a further grant of leave. In Odisho v The Queen,73 the police said that the \nwitness (the victim of the shooting that was the subject of the trial) had told them \nthat the accused was present when he was shot, but the witness said at trial that \nthat claim was an invention by the police. After the trial judge, relying on all three \nwings of section 38(1), granted the prosecution leave to cross-examine the victim, the prosecution asked him if he was lying, suggested that he was scared of the accused because of threats he received, and put that he was angry at the police for a variety of reasons. The three judges of the NSW Court of Criminal Appeal split three ways on whether these questions required leave under section 38(3). Justice Price held that none of the questions required leave, because ‘questions by the Crown of the victim about his possible motives to change his position were necessarily indirectly relevant to the facts-in-issue, particularly as he was the victim of the shooting’. Justice Bellew disagreed in the case of the third set of questions (about anger at the police), which \n\nwere, in my view, impermissibly wide ranging. Even when the observations of Heydon JA in Le are afforded their most liberal interpretation, it remains the case that those questions went solely to the victim’s credibility. If such wide ranging questioning was allowed to proceed on the basis that it was covered by a grant of leave under s 38(1), s 38(3) would have little or no work to do.\n\nBoth judges held that there was no miscarriage of justice because the importance \nof the victim’s credibility meant that the trial judge would have given leave under \nsection 38(3) anyway. However, Hamill J disagreed, holding that the second set of \nquestions (about fear of the accused, or friendship with him) also required leave, again because otherwise ‘it is difficult to see what work is left for sub-s 38(3) to do’, and that leave would not have inevitably been given on that topic, because exploring whether or not the accused threatened the victim simply to address the victim’s credibility may have been unfair to the accused.\n\nRegardless, the questions cannot go beyond the scope of the leave granted by the \ntrial judge. As it happens, in Asling, the judge permitted only a very limited series of questions: first, a repeat of the earlier question about whether the accused told the witness where the car was; second, a question to ensure that the witness no longer remembered what the accused said; third, taking the witness to the specific part of his earlier statement where he reported that the accused said the vehicle was in a laneway. According to Kaye JA:\n\nSuch limited cross-examination would not be unfair to the accused. Indeed, in some ways, it might even redound to his advantage, but it would certainly not be unfair because [his counsel] would then, I consider, have ample opportunity to cross-examine witness P about the differences in his memory about that matter and about associated issues.74\n\nIn Meyer (a pseudonym) v The Queen,75 the Court held that a prosecutor could not question a witness (the defendant’s wife) about her meetings with defence lawyers, as they were not relevant to why she testified in ways that were inconsistent with her earlier statement to the police.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [12012, 279, 2701, 1467, 21085, 1119, 220, 17, 20, 12756, 3501, 11, 10282, 304, 61182, 323, 5048, 271, 17, 13, 17, 13, 16, 13, 18, 1230, 69, 25998, 480, 27723, 271, 32, 4614, 5990, 6738, 264, 11298, 1576, 1340, 16552, 973, 429, 279, 11298, 686, 48162, 304, 264, 1616, 429, 8609, 279, 4614, 748, 1142, 13, 1988, 419, 374, 537, 2677, 279, 1142, 11, 438, 1045, 9677, 525, 8372, 2673, 53017, 311, 1618, 3654, 27723, 13, 1597, 7025, 279, 11298, 748, 23468, 10335, 705, 10476, 279, 1008, 3108, 11, 476, 1660, 537, 1753, 1492, 311, 5489, 382, 785, 13794, 5904, 13240, 28690, 264, 5473, 311, 1910, 264, 4614, 504, 279, 13475, 312, 7458, 389, 23568, 304, 10178, 421, 279, 11298, 279, 4614, 2598, 20027, 894, 315, 2326, 4682, 738, 700, 304, 3772, 220, 18, 23, 7, 16, 7731, 32, 4614, 879, 2598, 264, 11298, 1231, 11, 448, 279, 5274, 315, 279, 5473, 11, 3405, 279, 11298, 11, 438, 3498, 279, 4614, 1033, 