INFO 03-28 14:04:11 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-1716a97d705a4dff9cba3ae0d10df1fc-0: prompt: 'Please summarize this list of outcomes discussed into 15 themes 1. Supplied\n 1. Digital Services is generating revenues outside of Fabric in excess of $XXX\n 2. Digital Services is over $1Bn and growing YOY faster than our Data Center business\n 3. Our Partner Ecosystem is thriving with over XXX partners, YYY integrations, ZZZ use cases \n 4. Customer Feedback from Developers and Traditional Customers on our new offerings is excellent, we have greater than XXX transactional NPS \n 5. Our company valuation continues to grow as investors buy into the growth story of our platform offerings\n 6. Customers are able to engage our services in a self-service model with a pay-for-usage billing model \n\n2. Brandi\n · Adoption rates have grown at XX rate. (I think we need an adoption metric to balance revenue….increased adoption may be leading indicator of revenue growth and enough to keep us investing. \n · Customers and partners are able to engage our services in a self-service model with a pay-for-usage billing model\n · Our partner ecosystem is activated across all products and services.\n \n3. Bruce\n · Increase yield per cabinet from X to Y (If we commit to a number we will innovate to get there. I don’t believe we can meaningfully grow yield without innovation, and we don’t know exactly what innovation is required. This will be a forcing function) \n · Interconnection as % of revenue growing from X to Y (same principle as above)\n \n4. Charles\n \n5. Jon\n · Digital services is generating revenues outside of colo-related interconnection (exclude Fabric, IX, EC, MC) of > 500M.\n · Digital services is growing attach-rate/adoption by existing or new customers to 100+ G2000 enterprises, or 1000+ paying customers (over $1k MRR)\n · Our partner ecosystem is driving new incremental, low/no-touch acquisitions of > 20% of the partner deal mix.\n · Our company valuation shows multiple expansion based on the growth/revenue opportunity of digital services being valued as high-growth cloud-like valuations additive to the core.\n \n6. Justin\n · Digital services revenue from PLG/developer-led sales motion surpasses that of sales-led.\n · X% (e.g. >50%) attach rate across digital services (more than half of DS customers use at least 2 digital services)\n · Y% (e.g. > 25%) attach rate between data center services and digital services\n · Lower marginal cost of development for each new service\n · From product-centric to “experience-centric” development model (from linear product curation & value creation to exponential multi-sided value creation)… Platforms can enable an “inverted” business model, where the value could be derived primarily from a community (e.g. developers and technology partners) driving down the marginal cost of new products or services to virtually zero.\n · Developers love, praise and promote Platform Equinix (see diagrams for reference)\n\n \n \n \n \n \n7. Karl\n · …digital services active user grows to Multiple thousands in 2 years time\n · …PLG drives \n · Attach rate of digital services to existing Equinix customers reaches 50% by 2025\n · NPS of digital users attains critical industry benchmark threshold of 60 by EOY24\n · Industry Analysts readily includes us in the Cloud IaaS category\n \n8. Keith\n · I think it will be a multi-faceted view on success and outcomes. Yes, revenue growth will be important, but I think more important will be % of customers adopting digital services, number of services by deployment or customer, unit and total profitability as expressed in dollars with benefit to AFFO and AFFO per share, and rate of acceleration. Cash flow will create company valuation appreciation. Also, I think scale of infrastructure – number of countries, markets and buildings – will make a huge difference too. Speed of deployment will also be a tell-tale sign of success, which will determine the slope of the curve. \n \n9. Kurt\n · Point of lack of clarify, even in the example it defines the platform mostly in reference to digital. I think our platform is a platform as we have a full continuum that supports metal, to hybrid, to full colo. There is dissonance in the statements above. Platform is not digital alone.\n 1. When we have an MVP that supports customers in gaining access to the full suite of Equinix services through one or more methods of interaction (i.e. self service to full service) and multiple methods of purchase (direct or indirect). [Note, I didn’t say fully integrated billing, fully integrated portals, etc. But, we do need to have thoughtful ways to delivery a consistent look, feel and customer journey for the set of customers we want).\n \n 2. We have a fully developed self service capability for digital products across the full spectrum of digital products.\n 3. We have solved the issue of traditional colo products being sold digitally. What I mean by this is for colo customers who buy fabric, we need to make this easy within the environment they live in. Reality is, we need our colo customers to buy a lot of digital for digital to grow at the pace we want. Some of them may never by metal, but we certainly need it super easy to buy fabric and requiring them to move to a completely different system to buy the connectivity defeats the purpose. If they have to contract for fabric separately, why wouldn’t they just buy the same service from a telecom they already have a contract with?\n \n 4. Have defined our target customer\n \n10. Mike\n · Digital Services is generating X revenues per year. (I am fine with it being inclusive of Fabric)\n · We have co-built at least three products in the market with leading companies.\n · Customers can procure our digital services in a self-service model with a pay for usage billing model.\n · We have created a sustainable support model for digital services.\n \n11. Milind\n\n12. Nicole \n · People/Workforce/Brand:\n · The workforce can clearly articulate the company vision. \n · Brand identity is shifting in the market from data center company (space and power) to platform company. Metrics around reach, impressions, community engagement, style of customers hitting the platform, etc.. \n \n · Growth and Bookings:\n · Strategic value of the platform seen through net new customer growth of X percent, current customer growth across digital services of X percent (think measuring cross sell up sell) . \n · Scale and growth being driven through key synergies with critical ecosystem partners. Think Dell, HPE, and VMW (as examples). We would have have clear goals/targets between the companies for growth, and have successful product partnerships/ co-branding. IE: Dell drives “X” number of customers and or revenue to the Equinix platform. \n · Digital services hits $2B in revenue in 2 years (by end of 2025) and is growing at software market pace. Users would be consuming at rate exponentially higher than current state. \n · Sustainability efforts are clearly defined and part of overall strategic mission. (thus, not slowing down sales and ability to pursue deals that require sustainability gates). Should we consider this as a potential service revenue stream in future state? I know there will be specific sustainability targets and expectations by region so we should build it in now to our metrics/strategic approach. \n · Systems and Tools: \n · We have successfully implemented key shifts in our own internal digital transformation to provide a delightful experience for customers. For example, \n 1) Automation is in place to enable the “pull” of the platform in instead of the “push” it is today. Measure through metrics like employee productivity metrics, percentage of AI enabled tools, return on digital investments, Revenue/bookings from digital technology, ROI on digital/architecture investments. \n 2) Our architecture is designed for software style services and a simplified/seamless user experience. Similar metrics above could measure progress here I would think. \n · We have unified interfaces and solutions instead of puzzle pieces we are trying to stitch together. We’ve built a delightful experience for supporting our customers that want to buy multiple services together. This could be reflected through our NPS scores perhaps? \n \n13. PVC\n · I believe all 6 of the above matter. Tougher to assign numbers or an expectation of 4 thru 6 in an outcome as yet, but some form of measured progress will need to happen\n \n14. Raouf\n · Enterprise use cases become common and repeatable. \n · Direct Sales team equipped to sell to the Enterprise. \n · Customer experience is validated by customers. The on-demand model is scalable. \n · PLG takes off and grows as a “channel” to growth. \n · Clear product roadmap and capabilities in place. \n · The right product architecture and unit cost model to scale effectively. \n · Scalable service model for Enterprise and PLG support. Robust self-service where customers value it. \n · Market recognition of our position in the market as supported by customers. \n \n \n15. Ryan\n 1. Digital Services is generating $3B in X years.\n 2. Internally, Equinix S&B costs have costs are scaling, under-indexing to revenue growth. \n 3. The market understands and attributes distinctive value to the parts (DS, DCS, xScale) and the sum of the parts (Equinix). \n \n16. Scott\n · I do not understand the premise or intent of the Phase 1 questions. I don\'t think a platform vision is "proven" at the time it is ready to scale (it is only proven in retrospect, years later). You have to make it scale, and while you’re doing so, you encounter blockers every year or 18 months which you must overcome if you wish to continue scaling. If you fail to overcome those blockers (and evolve the platform based on market experience and evolution), it stops scaling. It is a continuum, not a point in time. So I\'m not sure what we\'re asking here. Are we asking the thresholds we need to meet at various arbitrary stages of growth, or to tackle particular challenges or take particular actions? If so, I’d argue $500M revenue and 25% CAGR without good BU/GTM alignment is a pretty clear indicator this has legs.\n\n · In any event, at each stage of growth, we can expect increases in metrics such as:\n ○ Usage growth by product \n ○ Revenue and revenue growth and/or profitability/losses\n · By business line\n · By ecosystem partner (reselling our offerings)\n ○ Degree of sales enablement (# DS sales certifications earned, % of reps whose DS deals comprise y% of their revenue) \n ○ Degree of sales ease (# people who have to touch each deal, average deal time by revenue band, % revenue from PLG)\n ○ Support systems: We can\'t scale without a simple, effective and measurable support experience for users and customers. We should measure the key metrics and outcomes (by persona type (describing product affinity): NPS and NPS-T , by product: mean time to resolution, mean number of handoffs to resolution) against the best in the industry.\n ○ Marketing: % of marketing budget allocated to DS; CAC/LTV by products\n ○ Consumption of our services by ecosystem partners, by each major category of ecosystem partner\n ○ Community adoption (for open source offerings)\n · The point, though, is that these targets will change every year. I don\'t understand what threshold we\'re seeking to quantify.', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [5501, 62079, 419, 1140, 315, 19554, 14078, 1119, 220, 16, 20, 21386, 220, 16, 13, 6299, 3440, 19