INFO 03-28 14:04:24 [logger.py:39] Received request cmpl-fb4efde9ac9e48409a037b9842f4b6c5-0: prompt: 'Summarize this text:"Logos and theoria become the driving forces of the new scientific mentality. The\nexpansion of commerce, the economy and socio-political contacts creates a greater\nneed for knowledge and skills. Erudition becomes a ‘craft’ in itself, and we speak of\n‘sages’ or wise men (sophoi, according to tradition they were seven in number), who,\nbased on their theoretical knowledge, served society in numerous ways. These sages\nconduct research in geography, agricultural science, economics, the state structure,\nup to and including the most general and abstract problems. After the desacralisation\nof nature the explanation of nature and the cosmos is among their most important\ntasks. Thus, the first philosophers are natural philosophers. They study nature (phusis,\nlit. ‘growth’) as an organism that is self-sustaining, and that should therefore be\nexplained through principles that are themselves part of nature. Hence, there is no\nneed for external factors or supernatural forces.\n The greatest source of fascination to these natural philosophers is the\ndiscovery that they can explain the order of nature in a rational way. The ‘cosmos’\n25\n(literally ‘ornament,’ ‘showpiece’) is itself a rationally ordered logos. Through the\nlogical power of thought, the first philosophers uncover a logic that exists in nature\nitself, and from which nature derives its order.\n The explanatory model of the first philosophers is materialistic. They look for\nthe (material) first principle, the ‘primordial matter’ of which the whole universe\nconsists. According to Thales of Miletus this primordial matter is water, according\nto Anaximenes air. How exactly this first principle is interpreted by various\nphilosophers, is not so important from a cultural-historical perspective. What is\nimportant, however, is the presupposition that there has to be such a first principle.\nIf we take the example of Anaximander’s system, the contrast with Hesiod’s\nexplanation becomes immediately clear:\nAfter him Anaximander, who had been his [Thales’s] associate, said that the\nInfinite was solely responsible for the coming-to-be and the passing-away of\nthe universe. Anaximander states that the various heavens have been secreted\nout of this Infinite, as more generally have been all the worlds, which are\ninfinite in number. He declared that passing-away and (much earlier) comingto-be take place as they all repeat a cycle from infinite time. He says that the\nearth is cylindrical in shape, and has a depth such as to be a third of its breadth.\nHe says that what is generative of hot and cold from the eternal was separated\noff at the coming-to-be of this world, and that from this a sort of ball of flame\ngrew round the air surrounding the earth, like the bark round a tree. When this\nhad been torn off and shut up in certain rings, the sun, moon, and stars came\ninto existence. (Plutarch, Stromata, fr. 179, 11-25) (Trans. F. H. Sandbach)\nThere is no longer any mention of starry Ouranos who covers his mother Gaia, nor\nof Erebos or Pontos, but only of strictly natural forces through which nature unfolds.\nFurthermore, those forces reveal a logic that can be uncovered through the logic of\nhuman comprehension.\n26\n The first interest of philosophical rationality was the explanation of nature,\nwhich gave rise to the development of cosmology. In the writings of two\nphilosophers of this period, Parmenides of Elea (c. 515 – c. 440 BC) and Heraclitus\nof Ephesus (born c. 543 BC), we witness the emergence of yet another tendency.\nWith them, the discovery of an inherent order in reality leads to a spectacular\nreflection on Being, and how it relates to the world of Becoming.\nHeraclitus: ‘everything flows’\nStarting from the idea that true reality is to be found in the chaotic jumble of\nphenomena, Heraclitus makes his famous assertion: ‘everything flows, nothing\nremains’ (panta rhei kai ouden menei). The world is subject to a permanent flux, which,\nfor example, makes it impossible to step into the same river twice. Precisely because\nof this continuous change, the world can exist as a cosmos. Heraclitus was convinced\nthat the harmony of the cosmos does not lie in the neutralisation of opposites, but\nthat the tension between opposites is essential to the order of the world. ‘War is\nfather of all’, he states: a bow or a lyre can only exist on account of the unity created\nby the permanent tension between the frame and the string. So, reality is not only\ncontinuously in motion, it is also the result of endless conflicts between opposites.\nThe cosmos is the result of a continually shifting balance between opposing forces.\nParmenides: ‘being is’\nIn many respects, Parmenides is Heraclitus’ opposite. Not only is his native city Elea\n(Southern Italy) geographically far removed from Ephesus (Asia Minor); his\ndoctrines, too, are radically opposed to those of his older contemporary (whose work\nhe probably never knew). Parmenides was the first to thematise being in itself, as the\nunique and unchanging foundation of the many beings. That way, the question of\nbeing was posed for the first time, and immediately in its most radical form. With\n27\ndisarming simplicity he asserts: ‘being is, not-being is not’. This insight may appear\nsimple, but its consequences are enormous.\n In his didactic poem On Nature, in which Parmenides relates how he is\nconveyed to the palace of Light by the daughters of the Sun and receives a revelation\nfrom the Goddess, the author gives his readers the choice between two ways: either\n‘that it is, and that it is impossible that it is not’, or ‘that it is not, and that it is necessary\nthat it is not’. The two positions are irreconcilable, but the problem is that ordinary\nmortals did not see the dilemma. As ‘two-headed’ creatures they are governed by the\nsenses, and out of ignorance they act as if there is no problem, as if both ways are\nopen simultaneously. Parmenides, on the other hand, urged by his Goddess, radically\nchooses the first way. Rather than allow himself to be governed by the deceptive\nsenses, he will only trust the coherent, intelligent argument of the logos. The\nimportance of this choice can hardly be overrated: it is a decisive moment in the\nhistory of Western philosophy.\n Thus, Parmenides resolutely chooses the way of being, and takes it to its\nextreme consequences: (1) ‘Being is’ implies, in the first place, that being cannot\ncome into being; where would it come from? It cannot come from nowhere, and if\nit comes from somewhere, this means that being already had to precede, which\nreconfirms the first claim that ‘being is’. On the other hand, being cannot perish,\nsince it cannot possibly ‘cancel’ itself. Nor can it transform into something else, since\nthen it would, to a certain extent, not be, which is also impossible. Being is, in other\nwords, unchanging and imperishable. (2) Moreover, being is indivisible; there are no\ndegrees of being: something is or it is not. Indeed: qua being (that is: simply and solely\nconsidered in its capacity of ‘being’) all that is, is identical. Any perceived difference\nbelongs to the order of the misleading senses. Being itself, on the other hand, is an\nundifferentiated continuum. (3) Being is motionless and limited. That this is the case\ncan be deduced from the foregoing. After all, if there is nothing outside being, being\nhas to be completely separate from what is not. Not-being cannot penetrate the\nindivisible continuum of being, and, vice versa, being cannot move itself towards\n28\nnot-being. (4) Being is perfect. For it is not the result of a process of becoming. If it\nis, it is fully realized. This also immediately explains why being has to be limited: it is\nfully unfolded, and does not have the possibility of going any further; in other words,\nit has reached its limit. (5) Finally, Parmenides states that being also has to be\nspherical. If we accept that it is perfect and limited, it has to be identical everywhere\nand in all directions. Hence the conclusion that the sphere is the only conceivable\nshape that being can assume.\n It may be clear from this last deduction that for Parmenides being is still\ncompletely permeated by materiality. Despite the enormous level of abstraction his\nthought has reached, there is still no purely abstract concept of being. For this,\nParmenides was criticized by Aristotle, who, in his treatise On the Heavens, writes :\nBut however excellent their [Parmenides and his followers] theories may\notherwise be, anyhow they cannot be held to speak as students of nature.\nThere may be things not subject to generation or any kind of movement, but\nif so they belong to another and a higher inquiry than the study of nature.\nThey, however, had no idea of any form of being other than the substance of\nthings perceived; and when they saw, what no one previously had seen, that\nthere could be no knowledge or wisdom without some such unchanging\nentities, they naturally transferred what was true of them to things perceived.\n(Aristotle, On the Heavens, 298b11-24) (Trans. J. L. Stocks)\nAristotle’s criticism boils down to this: Parmenides’ analyses are presented within the\nfield of natural philosophy, but they actually belong to another philosophical\ndiscipline: metaphysics. Natural philosophy studies reality insofar as it is concrete\nand changeable, whereas metaphysics studies the unchanging principles that underlie\nit. Parmenides’ concept of being remains tied to the being of perceptible things (as\npart of natural philosophy), and does not concern being-in-itself. Of course, this\ncriticism is largely anachronistic, but it helps to interpret Parmenides’ rather\n29\nconfusing description of being as a sphere. Parmenides’ notion of being is a\nmetaphysical ‘given’ that is not yet fully recognized as such. This is what Aristotle,\nwith great lucidity, understood.\n Nevertheless, the importance of Parmenides for Western philosophy remains\ninvaluable. The question that will concern philosophy from now on is raised in a\nspectacular way: with a radical choice in favour of reason and against the deceptive\nappearance of the senses, Parmenides forces his readers to take a position concerning\nthe inescapable logic he uses to analyse being.\n Thus, at the beginning of philosophy, the eternal opposition is put forward in\nall clarity: universal permanence, the unity of abstract being versus universal\ntransience, the plurality of concrete reality. All later thinkers found themselves facing\nthis opposition, and have tried to position themselves somewhere between the two. "', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.1, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=True, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None, extra_args=None), prompt_token_ids: [9190, 5612, 551, 419, 1467, 2974, 2201, 436, 323, 279, 10782, 3635, 279, 9842, 8437, 315, 279, 501, 12344, 48295, 13, 576, 198, 4580, 10501, 315, 35654, 11, 279, 8584, 323, 40489, 53996, 31260, 18508, 11450, 264, 7046, 198, 17046, 369, 6540, 323, 7361, 13, 9740, 661, 680, 9044, 264, 3369, 7712, 527, 304, 5086, 11, 323, 582, 6468, 315, 198, 42641, 1134, 527, 476, 23335, 2953, 320, 82, 5127, 6728, 11, 4092, 311, 13815, 807, 1033, 8094, 304, 1372, 701, 879, 345, 29939, 389, 862, 31787, 6540, 11, 10223, 8232, 304, 12114, 5510, 13, 4220, 274, 1134, 198, 76652, 3412, 304, 53142, 11, 28049, 8038, 11, 27889, 11, 279, 1584, 5944, 345, 454, 311, 323, 2670, 279, 1429, 4586, 323, 8115, 5322, 13, 4636, 279, 939, 580, 3461, 7923, 198, 1055, 6993, 279, 1614